11-20 | Alex chooses A after council Q (library useful for sources, not its number answers: linked questions contradict): no daily wrapper yet; link related questions first, a $0 check then a few-cents probe; the dependency stretch D533–D542 stays a provisional candidate | in force | `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1069:| D725 | 2026-11-22 | Council round 45/45b (Alex: find the design cause of unreliable numbers): related number questions have no shared, checked calculation — prose answers code cannot check, one question judged alone, split inputs; recommended: a new judge version judging related families in one call with labelled figures and identity/arithmetic checks, \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim, a $0 gate then a 36-call paid gate | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1071:| D727 | 2026-11-22 | judging-v13 $0 gate: design READY at council 46e; gate runs 1–6 (41 items, 15/15 mutants, one stored family rendered and checked); council 46f–46j GATE FAIL on relabelling and routing variants; open: scenario pairing, per-scenario same base, and whether the judge sees its earlier forecasts while calculating (changes D198; Alex's choice) | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1073:| D729 | 2026-11-22 | judging-v13 $0 gate passes (council 46ad): 87/87 under three hash seeds, 47/47 mutants, one stored family rendered and checked; next: the paid gate's sender and packets at $0 through the council, then Alex's ceiling (36 calls, ~$0.2–0.4) | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/GATE-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1075:| D731 | 2026-11-22 | judging-v13 paid-gate harness ready (council 47h): first attempt scored on both arms, retries diagnostic; packets hold the scored answers only and commit to their mapping; one-to-one reconciliation; sealed readings, one verdict; rehearsal 63/63, mutants 33/33; 36 first attempts ~$0.17 (≤$0.39); next: Alex names the ceiling (proposed $0.50) | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1077:| D733 | 2026-11-22 | judging-v13 paid gate STOP: all 72 answers refused ($0.37 of $0.50), 0 of 90 outcomes named a figure; the format's rules lived in the check, not the request (7 field descriptions), and some checks refuse right answers; process error: the $0 answer was written knowing the check; council 48: bounded repair + a $0 blind conformance test, or close v13 — Alex's choice | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1079:| D735 | 2026-11-22 | (superseded by D737) Council 49–50: the v13 repair is finished to a line (at most two more review rounds: wording and cases, then the sent request and checks; then the blind test once; a serious issue after the second goes back to Alex); shrinking to six checks set aside (drops C8's segment-into-total guard; exceeds D734); design v2 | superseded by D737 | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/REPAIR-DESIGN.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1080:| D736 | 2026-11-22 | Council 51: v2 wording review finds as many serious issues as round 49 (unstated C1–C9 rules, counterexamples to every check change, readers grading only refusals); Codex: fix and use the last round; Claude: back to Alex now (one mechanism, round after round); goes to Alex | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r51.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1082:| D738 | 2026-11-22 | v13s build rule (council 52–53): code refuses a named defect only where the calculation's structure decides it (direct part+whole sum, two forecast-basis figures or a bypassed shared forecast, part above whole on recomputed values, estimate/forecast at or under the reference, plus S1/S2/S6/S7); indirect forms recorded for readers, who mark the five defects on every reply (an accepted reply with a reader-marked defect stops the blind test); round 53's false refusals fixed | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1083:| D739 | 2026-11-22 | v13s built: council 54 fixes (whole-expression sources, values over ranged leaves, linked part/whole, period-aware S8, adopted forecasts only, stricter structure, a hardened blind runner); cases 100/100, mutants 38/38, rehearsal 19/19, old harness unchanged; the blind test runs now and its result goes to Council B | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1084:| D740 | 2026-11-22 | v13s blind test STOP: S1 refused a right answer (\"from $35B to $170B\" read as a range; Claude's round-54 change) and an accepted reply carried a reader-marked two-values-for-Q3 defect (R06; same pattern judged clean in R12); 12/12 schema-valid, 11/12 accepted; council 55: three reader rulings to Alex (growth benchmark, computed market reference, total capex as data-center capex), fix S1, re-run the blind test once | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1086:| D742 | 2026-11-22 | v13s re-run STOP: S4 refused a sound answer that computed a guidance rate straight into Q4 (request pulled three ways); council 56–56c: blanket and narrowed exemptions both let a reset to guidance publish, so S4 stays and the request states one rule (guidance informs the pace; the pace is an estimate naming its benchmark; the quarter's dollar value is the one forecast); refusal review registers sound and stated apart; two reader splits disclosed (O's $18B reading, prose numbers); third $0 blind run next | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS.md:1087:| D743 | 2026-11-22 | third v13s blind run: writers clean (12/12 accepted, D742 rule followed by all six P writers); STOP as written on R01 (both readers marked a range across a rung edge; both adjudicators rejected under the schema's range clause; cause: reader brief lacks that clause, Claude's error); disclosures R07 unsupported 90% share, O $18B split, R01 total-capex reference; council 57: ask Alex to authorize a blinded re-read of the 12 replies with the corrected brief and controls; Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\" | in force | `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` |\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10380:  library PARTLY: for sources and milestones, not for its number answers, because linked number questions contradict each other\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10957:## D725 · 2026-11-22 · Council round 45/45b: the number answers fail because related numbers have no shared, checked calculation; one judge version and one test recommended to Alex\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:10962:- **The record:** `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/` (`RESULT.md`).\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11004:  - Until the number answers pass, a first release shows number questions with their sources and \"Forecast unavailable\": the band, the\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11010:- **Design:** `docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/DESIGN.md`, v1 to v9. Council rounds 46 to 46e reached READY FOR THE $0 GATE on v5.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11057:- **Verdict:** GATE PASS STANDS (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r46ad.md`, `GATE-RESULT.md`).\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11085:- **What was built (D730):** `research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py` (plan, send, recover, packets, verdict, replay),\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11125:  not stopped). Run folder `poc/data/judging-v13-paid-gate-2026-11-22/`. Record: `research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE-RESULT.md`.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11166:  - The alternative is to close judging-v13. poc-v118 stays the product (D690), and the number answers stay a known defect.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11187:- **Round 49** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r49.md`) reviewed the repair design v1 and found it not ready:\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11212:- **Round 51** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r51.md`) reviewed design v2's exact wording and cases. It reproduced all 72\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11260:- **Rounds 52 and 53** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r52.md`, `codex-r53.md`) reviewed the small format within D737's\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11292:- **Round 54** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r54.md`) reviewed the code. It found real defects, each now fixed with a case\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11327:- **Result** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22/`; $0 at the\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11369:## D742 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s re-run stops on one sound answer refused by S4; council 56–56c keeps S4 and makes the request state one benchmark rule; a third $0 blind run follows\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11371:- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md`, run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/`):\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11408:     The stop lines are unchanged (`SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D742]).\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11416:## D743 · 2026-11-22 · The third v13s blind run stops on the reader brief; the writer side is clean; council 57 asks Alex to authorize a re-reading\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11418:- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md`, run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`):\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11421:    - all six P-family writers recorded the Q4 pace as an estimate naming its benchmark (D742's rule);\n     492 AGENTS.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md && nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '162,390p' && nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r57.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13s blind test, third run after D742: result (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D742])\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP on one registered line, as written** (council round 57 confirms): \"an accepted reply with a named defect (marked by both readers …)\".\n     4\t- **R01 (S, Claude writer).** Both readers marked \"broken structure\" on F14: the outcome is a range of $679.25–745B, and rung 4\n     5\t  ($700–800B) does not hold all of it.\n     6\t- **Both adjudicators reject that mark.** The schema the writer was given says the rung is \"for a range outcome, a band it overlaps,\n     7\t  with the reason in reasons\". The answer gives that reason: \"the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4\". The code (S6) checks\n     8\t  overlap.\n     9\t- **The cause is my reader brief.** Its definition of broken structure reads \"a rung whose band does not hold the outcome\", without the\n    10\t  schema's range clause. Both readers applied the brief as written. No earlier run had a range that crossed a rung edge, so the gap was\n    11\t  not exercised before.\n    12\t\n    13\tEverything else in the run is clean:\n    14\t- **Writers:** 12 of 12 schema-valid, 12 of 12 accepted, 0 refusals, so there was no refusal review.\n    15\t- **Readers and adjudicators:** six packets drew a disagreement. Every disputed mark was settled, and every one was rejected by both\n    16\t  adjudicators. No defect was upheld, and no split remains.\n    17\t- **Every case has a usable reply.** The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R02 and R11).\n    18\t- **The D742 rule was followed by every P-family writer.** All six recorded the Q4 pace as an estimate naming its benchmark, then\n    19\t  computed Q4 from it. None computed a guidance rate straight into the calculation.\n    20\t\n    21\t**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`, $0 at the provider. All Codex writers read only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n    22\t`schema.json`. The tally is in `TALLY.json`.\n    23\t\n    24\t## 1. Writers' first answers (`CHECKED.json`)\n    25\t\n    26\t| Case | Claude writer | Codex writer |\n    27\t|---|---|---|\n    28\t| P (joint) | v4 416.3, v8 386.1, both rung 2 | v4 420.9, v8 391.0, both rung 2 |\n    29\t| P-v4 | 419.3, rung 2 | 416.3, rung 2 |\n    30\t| P-v8 | 387.4, rung 2 | 388.1, rung 2 |\n    31\t| S (CY2026) | 679.25–745, rung 4 | 670.5, rung 3 |\n    32\t| O | 9–20, rung 1 | 16, rung 1 |\n    33\t| E | 37, rung 3 | 34, rung 3 |\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n    36\t\n    37\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    38\t- R01 broken structure (both readers' marks), explained above;\n    39\t- R01 \"two forecasts\" (Microsoft backed out of the four-company tallies, a reader matter through `rests_on`);\n    40\t- two \"plus or minus 2%\" midpoint marks (R02, R09) and a rounding difference within tolerance (R08). In all, both adjudicators\n    41\t  rejected 8 marks covering 7 distinct allegations (F14 was marked twice);\n    42\t- R06's \"number misread\" claims on the $18B schedule and the Lancium tranche.\n    43\t\n    44\t**Support:**\n    45\t- **R07 (S, Codex writer) is settled \"not supported\".** Its rung rests on a 90% data-center share, while the passages put the share near\n    46\t  100%:\n    47\t  - e1947: more than $730B \"on AI infrastructure\" against $720–745B of capex;\n    48\t  - e2759 and e5376: NVIDIA's \"nearly $800 billion\" for the top five, written both as capex and as data-center capex;\n    49\t  - Meta's own figures: about 96%.\n    50\t  At 94% or more, the same $745B gives rung 4. The reply is accepted and publishable, but its rung does not follow from the evidence.\n    51\t  This is not a registered stop line (usable is counted per case, and S has R01). It is a real judgement error in an answer that would\n    52\t  publish.\n    53\t- **R06 (O, Claude writer) is split again** on the $18B reading: Codex says not supported, Claude says supported. This is the same\n    54\t  passage as in both earlier runs.\n    55\t- Every other forecast is supported by both readers or both adjudicators.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. What the test shows\n    58\t\n    59\t- **The writer-side contract holds.** In three runs, the request has moved from 11 of 12 accepted with a checker error (run 1), to 11 of\n    60\t  12 with a request contradiction (run 2), to 12 of 12 with no refusal (run 3). D742's rule was followed by all six writers it applies\n    61\t  to.\n    62\t- **The stop this time is in the measuring instrument.** The reader brief's definition is narrower than the format the writer was\n    63\t  given. Both adjudicators corrected it from the format rule.\n    64\t- **The readers did their job on substance.** They found the one reply whose decisive assumption is unsupported (R07). That is the\n    65\t  kind of error the paid re-send is meant to measure in gpt-6-luna.\n    66\t- **Council round 57 found two more points** (`codex-r57.md`):\n    67\t  - R01, the only usable S reply, marks Zacks's $720–745B of *total* capex as the market reference for *data-center* capex. Claude's\n    68\t    reader noticed it in its ground but filed no named defect.\n    69\t  - R11's prose offers \"$138 billion in Q4\" as reaching rung 1, but it gives $428.32B against the $431.2B edge. The forecast itself\n    70\t    sits in rung 2.\n    71\t- **Open disclosures:**\n    72\t  - O's $18B reading (split three runs running);\n    73\t  - wrong numbers in prose, which this run's adjudications did not raise;\n    74\t  - R07's unsupported share.\n    75\t\n    76\t## 4. The blinded re-read (D743, Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\"; `reread-packets/`, `reread-adjudication/`, `REREAD-TALLY.json`)\n    77\t\n    78\tFresh readers re-read the same 12 replies under the corrected range rule. Two controls were mixed in, under a fresh shuffle Q01–Q14.\n    79\t\n    80\t- **The range repair works.**\n    81\t  - C-disjoint was marked broken structure by both readers.\n    82\t  - C-overlap was marked by neither.\n    83\t  - R01 (now Q07), the range across a rung edge, was not marked broken structure by either reader.\n    84\t- **STOP again, as written: Q10 (P-v8, Codex writer).**\n    85\t  - Both readers marked \"number misread\" on F6: \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" given as its midpoint, 108.\n    86\t  - Both adjudicators rejected it. The schema the writer was given says \"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high\n    87\t    or its midpoint as value\".\n    88\t  - The same mark has been raised about 12 times across the four readings, by single readers until now. Every adjudication rejected it.\n    89\t- **The class.** The readers' packet carries the user text and the answer, but not the format rules the writer was given. The\n    90\t  adjudication packet carries them and its definitions say \"allowing what the format rules permit\". Both stops caused by the instrument\n    91\t  (the range, the midpoint) come from this one gap.\n    92\t- **C-overlap's other mark is my error.** Both readers marked \"two forecasts\" on C-overlap because my control kept E's original text\n    93\t  (\"85% … $37 billion\") beside the new range. The readers were right to flag the inconsistency. It does not touch the range rule the\n    94\t  control tests.\n    95\t- **Judgement noise:**\n    96\t  - S's Codex reply (90% share) was settled \"not supported\" by both adjudicators in the first reading, and is \"supported\" by both fresh\n    97\t    readers now.\n    98\t  - S's Claude reply (Q07) is split at both levels this time: Codex says not supported and upholds two marks; Claude rejects them.\n    99\t  - O's Claude reply (Q09) is split again.\n   100\t- **No settled defect.** Every adjudicated mark on Q09, Q10 and Q14 was rejected by both adjudicators. Q07's two marks split.\n   162\t## 5. The $0 blind test (registered; REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5 with rounds 51's fixes)\n   163\t\n   164\t**Inputs.** Each case's archive, call, question, ladder and unit are registered, and the build refuses a heading that differs.\n   165\t\n   166\t| Case | Archive / call | Key | Ladder edges ($bn) | Role |\n   167\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n   168\t| P (joint), P-v4, P-v8 (separate) | old-1 v4 8810, v8 8816 | NVIDIA revenue company FY2027; NVIDIA revenue Data Center FY2027 | 431.2/392/352.8/313.6; 400/350/300/250 | the pair; 79 quotes absent from the gate |\n   169\t| S | old-3 v6 9043 | four largest AI hyperscalers, data-center capex, combined, CY2026 | 500/600/700/800 | fresh: 41 of 50 quotes absent from the gate |\n   170\t| O | old-2 v29 8507 | NVIDIA equity investment commitments company end of FY2027 (the fiscal year-end balance the annual report publishes) | 25/50/75/100 | control |\n   171\t| E | old-3 v41 9071 | NVIDIA revenue Vera CPU FY2028 | 50/40/30/20 | control |\n   172\t\n   173\t**Part A, code, $0.** Each case asserts its intended rule and message. The schema's validity is checked first, so a schema error\n   174\tcannot mask the rule under test.\n   175\t- **A1, correct forms.** Each passes:\n   176\t  - a $4.5 billion ± 3% guidance as two sources with apply_growth ends;\n   177\t  - Q1 + Q2 = H1;\n   178\t  - FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4;\n   179\t  - a year-over-year rate on last year's quarter (recorded);\n   180\t  - whole × mix through share;\n   181\t  - \"over $300 billion\" with qualifier over;\n   182\t  - \"-3%\";\n   183\t  - \"$300 million or more\" with qualifier \"or more\";\n   184\t  - $1.2 trillion as 1200 USD bn;\n   185\t  - a range outcome overlapping its rung;\n   186\t  - one figure marked for two members (its own family);\n   187\t  - a computed external reference;\n   188\t  - an unresolved member;\n   189\t  - a conversion between quantities (recorded);\n   190\t  - \"not only still expects $20 billion\" as a source;\n   191\t  - 12% × 2x = 24%.\n   192\t- **A2, defects.** Each is refused by its named rule:\n   193\t  - **S1:** 108.5 for \"$108.0 billion\"; a qualifier dropped; one invented; \"-3%\" as 3; a number not in the quote; a passage's\n   194\t    summary number; trillions as 1.2 USD bn.\n   195\t  - **S2:** a plain wrong result; a wrong end of a range; division by zero; money × %; a three-input difference.\n   196\t  - **S3:** a part forecast above the whole for a listed quarter; an outcome above the whole's.\n   197\t  - **S8:** company + Data Center in one sum; Q2 + Q3 labelled H1; FY − Q1 labelled Q4.\n   198\t  - **S4:** two forecasts of one shared quarter; an outcome reaching guidance for a shared quarter beside the forecast.\n   199\t  - **S5:** a forecast beneath a reference; an estimate beneath a reference; a reported-only reference; both a marked reference and\n   200\t    a reason; neither.\n   201\t  - **S6:** no outcome marker; two; a marker on an unresolved member; an outcome key mismatch; a rung outside the ladder; a rung\n   202\t    whose band misses the outcome; at the band's upper edge.\n   203\t  - **S7:** value with low; a cycle; a dangling input; a passage id in inputs; a source with basis forecast; duplicate answers.\n   204\t- **A3, Luna's observed mistakes** from both journals, each encoded in v13s. Each is impossible under the schema or refused with a\n   205\t  reason the request states (the sentence is quoted):\n   206\t  - prose outcome, reference and evidence;\n   207\t  - unit spellings;\n   208\t  - value with range;\n   209\t  - a four-input difference;\n   210\t  - money × % through product;\n   211\t  - \"first half\";\n   212\t  - a passage id in inputs;\n   213\t  - a trillion outcome.\n   214\t\n   215\t**[52] Additions to Part A.**\n   216\t- **A1 adds:**\n   217\t  - the remainder company − Data Center = the rest;\n   218\t  - \"US$10 billion\", \"$96.2bn\", \"roughly twice\", \"between $105.84 billion and $110.16 billion\";\n   219\t  - \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" as a range and as a midpoint;\n   220\t  - a point Data Center forecast beside a company range (S3 holds);\n   221\t  - a final range estimate resting on a computed point of the same quantity (S4 holds).\n   222\t- **A2 adds:**\n   223\t  - Data Center × 1x added to company (S8);\n   224\t  - an estimate resting on both part and whole (recorded, not refused, and the record asserted);\n   225\t  - two Data Center Q4 figures on two paths (S4);\n   226\t  - Q3 guidance used directly beside a Q3 forecast (S4);\n   227\t  - an estimate itself marked as the reference (S5);\n   228\t  - a computed reference with basis forecast (S5);\n   229\t  - a declared value within tolerance whose recomputed value crosses the rung edge (S6 on the recomputed value);\n   230\t  - a part whose recomputed lowest value exceeds the whole's recomputed highest, while its declared value does not (S3);\n   231\t  - sum($108B, 2%) (S2 unit);\n   232\t  - division by [-1, 1] (S2);\n   233\t  - [-3, -2] × [2, 4] declared other than [-12, -4] (S2);\n   234\t  - a source as the outcome (S6);\n   235\t  - a figure id equal to a passage id (S7);\n   236\t  - \"plus or minus 2%\" copied as \"2%\" and used as +2% for the range (S1);\n   237\t  - a euro amount (S1).\n   238\t- **A3:** each mistake must be refused by the schema or **actually refused** by its stated rule (asserted). \"first half\" is refused by\n   239\t  S8 when summed. \"1.05 USD trillion\" fails the schema, and \"1050 USD bn\" passes.\n   240\t- **A4, the end to end rehearsal.** One v13s reply goes through the first attempt, the separate-arm reconciliation, the card render and\n   241\t  recovery. For each named defect, the defective answer fails its rule while its paired correct answer reaches the reader's card. The\n   242\t  old run's `results.json` reproduces from its journals with the ledger snapshot saved inside it.\n   243\t\n   244\t**[52] Readers.**\n   245\t- **What they get.** Both readers get the same complete shown passages, with the writer's identity and the checker's decision masked.\n   246\t- **What they mark, on every first reply:**\n   247\t  - whether each forecast is supported, naming the passage or assumption that puts it in its band;\n   248\t  - any of D737's five defects;\n   249\t  - broken structure.\n   250\t- **The wording review is separate.** Refusals are revealed only after that marking, for the review of whether the request states\n   251\t  the rule.\n   252\t- **An accepted reply with a named defect** (marked by both readers, or by the third reader) is a stop.\n   253\t- **INCOMPLETE** is judged against the inventory of 12 first replies and 24 readings plus the third readings due.\n   254\t\n   255\t**Part B, blind writers ($0 at the provider).**\n   256\t- **The writers.** Each of the six cases is written once by a fresh Claude subagent and once by a fresh read-only Codex session, 12\n   257\t  sessions in all. Each gets only that case's exact outgoing system text, user text and schema.\n   258\t- **The first outputs** go through `model.check_schema`, `from_answer` and `check` unedited.\n   259\t- **The readers.** Two readers who wrote neither the design nor the answers (a Claude subagent and a Codex session) grade **every**\n   260\t  answer, accepted or refused, against the shown passages. For each answer they record:\n   261\t  - for each refusal, whether the exact request states the condition (the sentence quoted) and whether the answer was right;\n   262\t  - for each accepted answer, whether each forecast is supported, and whether its recorded conversions or estimates decide its band.\n   263\t- **A third reader** settles disagreements.\n   264\t- **Usable** means accepted, publishable by `publish_decision`, and supported by the readers. For the joint case, both forecasts must\n   265\t  be supported in one reply.\n   266\t\n   267\t**Stop.** The test stops on any of these:\n   268\t- an A1 form refused;\n   269\t- an A2 defect not refused by its rule;\n   270\t- an A3 mistake neither impossible nor stated;\n   271\t- a Part B refusal whose condition the request does not state, or that refuses a right answer;\n   272\t- an INCOMPLETE run;\n   273\t- a case with zero usable first replies across both writers;\n   274\t- the joint case without both supported forecasts in one reply.\n   275\t\n   276\t**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\n   277\tD725's lines. Only that run measures whether luna can do it.\n   278\t\n   279\t**[D742] Registered before the third run (council rounds 56–56c).** The re-run stopped on one S4 refusal of a sound answer\n   280\t(`BLIND2-RESULT.md`). The cause was the request: the system text said to carry \"the guided growth forward\", while the schema allowed a\n   281\tguidance rate only as \"a stated benchmark for an estimate\" and the header required every path to go through the forecast. S4 is\n   282\tunchanged (rounds 56 and 56b showed that both a blanket and a narrowed exemption let a reset to guidance publish). The request now states\n   283\tone rule in the system text, the schema and the header: guidance informs the pace; the forecast pace is recorded as an estimate that\n   284\tnames its benchmark in `rests_on`; the quarter's dollar value is the one forecast figure. The rules for the third run:\n   285\t- **The stop lines are unchanged.**\n   286\t- **The refusal review** records two judgements apart for each refusal:\n   287\t  - sound: is the forecast and its reasoning financially sound?\n   288\t  - stated: does the exact request (system text, schema and user text) clearly state the condition?\n   289\t  A sound answer refused under a clearly stated condition still stops the run.\n   290\t- **The readers' brief** keeps Alex's ruling-1 sentence and adds: \"Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated\n   291\t  assumption, for a second dollar value of the quarter.\"\n   292\t- **Adjudication:**\n   293\t  - Where the two readers disagree, two adjudicators, one per family, read the packet. The packet now includes the system text.\n   294\t  - A point is settled when both agree.\n   295\t  - A settled defect on an accepted reply is a stop.\n   296\t  - A split is disclosed, not a stop.\n   297\t- **What each outcome sends where:**\n   298\t  - a fresh sound refusal sends the format back to council;\n   299\t  - an accepted reply confirmed to carry two values of a quarter sends the publication rule back to council;\n   300\t  - only a complete run that meets every line goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\n   301\t- **Part A for the change:**\n   302\t  - five new cases: the stated-estimate benchmark passes; four forms of the saved refusal or a guidance reset are refused by S4;\n   303\t  - one new mutant: S4's walk following `rests_on` must fail the stated-estimate case;\n   304\t  - `s4_replay.py` on the saved reply.\n   305\t\n   306\t**[D743] The blinded re-read of the third run (Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\"; council round 57), registered before any packet is built.**\n   307\t- **The one change.** The readers' brief and the adjudication header add the schema's range rule to \"broken structure\": for a range\n   308\t  outcome, \"a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons\" is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is.\n   309\t- **What is re-read.** The same 12 first replies of `judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22`. Writers are not re-run, and the request is\n   310\t  unchanged.\n   311\t- **Two blinded controls** are built from a copy of E's Claude reply. Its growth estimate becomes a range, giving an outcome of $36–43B,\n   312\t  which crosses the $40B edge.\n   313\t  - C-overlap names rung 3 and gives its reason. It must not be marked.\n   314\t  - C-disjoint names rung 4 ($20–30B), a band the range does not reach. It must be marked broken structure.\n   315\t  Controls are scored on the readers' marks, not on support.\n   316\t- **Blinding.** 14 packets under a fresh shuffle (ids Q01–Q14, key in `reread-key.json` beside, never in the packets). The readers are\n   317\t  fresh: one Claude subagent and one Codex session per packet. They see only their packet, never earlier votes, the key or the expected\n   318\t  result.\n   319\t- **Scoring:**\n   320\t  - The registered stop lines apply to the 12 replies exactly as before, with adjudication where the readers disagree.\n   321\t  - The repair fails if either control is read wrongly by both readers, or by the adjudication when the two readers split on it.\n   322\t  - Any registered stop on the 12 replies, including S losing its only usable reply, sends the result back to council.\n   323\t  - A clean re-read goes to Alex for the paid re-send, beside the third run's record and its disclosures.\n     1\t1. **Serious — The third run stopped under the rule as registered.** R01 forecast $679.25–745 billion and chose rung 4, which starts at $700 billion. Both readers marked the same defect on F14. That independently triggers the stop at §5 line 252. D742 kept that line and assigned adjudication to disagreements. The adjudicators reviewed F14 because the packet also contained disagreements, and both correctly rejected the F14 mark. Their finding explains the stop without reversing it. The reader brief omitted the range exception. The writer’s schema allowed an overlapping band with a stated reason, and R01 gave one. I replayed the reply in memory. Rung 4 published, and changing only the selection to the disjoint rung 5 triggered S6. [Registered stop](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:244) · [D742 procedure](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:279) · [Reader brief](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_test.py:169) · [Writer’s schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/schema.json:329) · [S6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:541) · [R01 adjudications](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R01.codex.json) · [Claude’s adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R01.claude.json)\n     2\t\n     3\t2. **Wording — The counts reconcile with two corrections to the report.** All 12 replies passed the schema and published on replay, with zero refusals. There were 24 first readings and 12 adjudication readings across six packets. Both adjudicators rejected all eight individual defect marks, covering seven distinct allegations because F14 was marked twice. R02 and R09 account for **two** midpoint marks. Five replies used the permitted midpoint form. “No split remains” applies to defect marks only. Across 14 forecasts, 12 were supported, R07 was unsupported, and R06 stayed split. Every case had a usable reply on those readings, and both joint replies supported both forecasts. The 109 Part A checks also passed on replay. [Checker results](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json) · [Tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:382) · [Report](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:13) · [Part A results](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small-cases.log:110)\n     4\t\n     5\t3. **Serious — R07 exposes a forecast that can publish without enough evidence. It adds zero registered stops.** The arithmetic is right. At $745 billion of total spending, the 90% share gives $670.5 billion, and a share of about 94% reaches rung 4. Both adjudicators found the evidence too weak to justify the cut to 90%. I agree with that narrower conclusion. Meta’s figures are for one company, the nearly $800 billion estimate covers five, and the four-company $730 billion figure measures AI infrastructure. Those are reasons to question 90%, with no firm lower bound for the exact four-company data-center share. R01 supplied the usable reply that S needed. Carry R07 forward as a disclosed example of what the eventual paid measurement needs to reveal. [R07 calculation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json:213) · [Source passages](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:29) · [R07 adjudications](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json:3) · [Claude’s adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.claude.json:1) · [Usability rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:263)\n     6\t\n     7\t4. **Serious — R01 has a separate problem that the range ruling leaves open.** It labels Zacks’s $720–745 billion of **all** capital spending as the market reference for **data-center** spending. The writer’s instructions ask for a reference for the question itself. Claude’s first reading noticed the mismatch in its explanation, and neither reader filed it as a named defect. The same outside tally also helps set the Microsoft estimate, so agreement with the tally adds little confidence. The adjudicators addressed that dependence when they rejected the two-forecasts mark. The reference mismatch adds zero stops to the recorded vote, but it matters because R01 is S’s sole usable reply. Fresh readers need to judge its substance as well as its range. [R01 reference](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json:137) · [Zacks passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:83) · [Writer’s reference instruction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/system.txt:4) · [First reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R01.claude.json:1) · [Adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R01.codex.json:17)\n     8\t\n     9\t5. **Minor — Two disclosures deserve more precise wording.** Both O replies depend on reading the $18 billion as funding from the July balance. R04 states the overlap and payment timing as estimates. R06 presents the $18 billion as the company’s schedule, and the adjudicators split on whether that reading supports the forecast. R11 also contains a prose error that a reader did catch. A $138 billion fourth quarter gives $428.32 billion for the year, below the $431.2 billion cutoff it was offered to illustrate. Its actual forecast still sits in rung 2, and the reader filed no named defect. I found no other confirmed registered stop. [O source](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/O/user.txt:36) · [R04 assumption](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/O/answers/codex.json:172) · [R06 adjudication](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R06.codex.json:3) · [R11 reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R11.claude.json:1)\n    10\t\n    11\t6. **Recommendation — Take the recorded STOP through Council B and ask Alex to authorize option (a).** Register the schema’s exact range rule in both reading instructions, then have fresh readers and adjudicators review all 12 original replies. Give them reshuffled IDs and access only to their packets. Keep prior votes, the answer key, and the expected result from them. Register the wording and scoring before release, with blinded controls for an explained overlap and a disjoint band. Reusing the replies can bias the people choosing the correction. Those controls and the full reread test whether the correction works beyond the case that prompted it. Option (b) would introduce fresh writer variation into a question about how the same answers were read. Option (c) is the right approval step, with the original STOP on the record. A clean reread can support a separate paid-run request to Alex. [Council B requirement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11318) · [Authority after a registered stop](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:339) · [Paid-run decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:267)\n    12\t\n    13\t   **Falsifier.** If the corrected reading rejects the valid overlap or accepts the disjoint control, the repair fails. If the reread hits any registered stop, including S losing its sole usable reply, the result stays with the council.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
