{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n --no-heading '.' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q*.{claude,codex}.json\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:3:    \"v29\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"no\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:5:      \"why\": \"e2620 reports $25 billion in commitments as of July 26. e2889 says NVIDIA had $18 billion committed for the rest of its fiscal year. The forecast subtracts all $18 billion from the July balance, but the passage does not say that the full amount was included in that balance or would be paid by year-end. The answer presents both assumptions as NVIDIA's payment schedule. The $9–20 billion forecast and rung 1 depend on that unsupported deduction.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:6:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:7:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:8:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:9:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:10:      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:12:      \"figures\": [\"F2\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:13:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:14:      \"why\": \"e2889 says it has \\\"$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.\\\" F2 copies the amount, scale, sign and qualifier correctly. The defect is the unsupported interpretation of what the $18 billion represents.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:15:    },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:16:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:17:      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:18:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:19:      \"figures\": [\"F10\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:20:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:21:      \"why\": \"The format requires the qualifier from the passage's quote. In e1919, \\\"up to\\\" modifies the $3 billion total, while the tranche is quoted as \\\"an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting\\\" conditions. F10 preserves the amount and identifies it as milestone-linked. Using the full tranche in the low estimate is a forecast assumption.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:22:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:23:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:24:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:3:    \"v41\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:5:      \"why\": \"Management's $20 billion FY2027 base and doubling outlook, broad planned Vera deployments, and CPU market growth support a strong FY2028 forecast. The answer explains why it discounts the outlook to 85% growth for execution risk and AMD competition. Applying that estimate to $20 billion gives $37 billion, within rung 3.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:6:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:7:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:8:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:9:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:10:      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:12:      \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:13:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:14:      \"why\": \"The format says the source value is 'the number as written,' stores 'more than' separately as the qualifier, and defines product as 'a × b × …'. F3 correctly computes 20 × 2 = 40 from the recorded values. The passage's 'more than double' makes $40 billion a threshold and leaves $50 billion or more possible, so the qualifier matters when interpreting the reference.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:15:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:16:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:17:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:3:    \"v8\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:5:      \"why\": \"The reported first half totals $164.27 billion in Data Center revenue (e109 and e5079). Applying the stated 16% and 15% sequential company revenue growth assumptions and the 93% and 93.5% Data Center shares yields about $388.08 billion for FY2027, within rung 2's $350 billion to below $400 billion band. The prior guidance beat and initial Rubin shipments support a Q3 above guidance (e3200 and e2693). Expected Q4 hyperscaler acceleration and supply constraints support further growth at a constrained pace (e3406 and e5067). The rising Data Center share and its role as the primary driver of Q3 growth support the assumed mix gains (e3399 and e5223). Morningstar's separate $361 billion estimate is directly quoted in e2295.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:6:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:7:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:8:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:9:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:10:      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:12:      \"figures\": [\"F6\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:13:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:14:      \"why\": \"F6 quotes e2652's full expression, '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%'. The format permits that expression to be 'given as low and high or its midpoint as value'. The $108 billion point is the midpoint.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:15:    },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:16:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:17:      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:18:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:19:      \"figures\": [\"F15\", \"F11\", \"F13\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:20:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:21:      \"why\": \"The listed inputs give 128.36 × 93.5 ÷ 100 = 120.0166. F15's reported 120.01 differs by $0.0066 billion and satisfies the rule that 'The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %)'.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:22:    },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:23:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:24:      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:25:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:26:      \"figures\": [\"F6\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:27:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:28:      \"why\": \"F6 preserves '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%' in quote_number and uses the permitted midpoint as value. The qualifier field is for 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or \\\\'or more\\\\' / \\\\'or less\\\\' just after)'. The quote has no such qualifier, so 'none' is correct.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:29:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:30:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:31:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v29\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The calculation follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions. e2620 gives 'equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026' (F1). e2889 is the company's statement on the same day (2026-08-26) that 'it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year'. The answer reads this as the part of the commitment book due in the rest of FY2027. That reading is an inference, but it is the natural one: the figure is tied to a time ('for the rest of its fiscal year'), was given alongside the reported balance, and is smaller than it. The answer states the reading openly ('the company's own schedule for the current half') and names what would reverse it ('a large part of the $18 billion slipping into next year'). F4 ($2-13 billion) is marked as the writer's own estimate, with its assumption written out. It counts the staged money in e1970 (SB Energy's second $1.5 billion), e1919 (Lancium's additional $1 billion) and e4648 (an Anthropic anchor of 'as much as $10 billion'). It leaves out deals paid at closing (e2207, e3474, e1957), and e2643 and e4487 support that choice. The arithmetic checks: 25 - 18 = 7; 7 + 2 = 9; 7 + 13 = 20. The range $9-20 billion lies wholly below the $25 billion boundary, so rung 1 holds the outcome. Reader 1 objects that the forecast 'has not established' a year-end balance below $25 billion. The system text rules that ground out: a rung is the most likely outcome, not a proved one, and '\\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay'. The answer also explains why Bank of America's $70 billion is a different measure: e4729's '>$42 billion' already bought plus the $25 billion still owed comes close to it.\"}},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.claude.json:2: \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.claude.json:3:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F2\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"A number misread needs the number, scale, sign or qualifier to differ from the quote. e2889's quote writes '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year' with no qualifier before the number. F2 records value 18, unit USD bn, quote_number '$18 billion', qualifier 'none', so all four match. Reader 1's complaint is about how the answer uses the figure, as a payment schedule that reduces the July 26 balance. That is a question of interpretation, judged under whether the member is supported. It is not a misread of the number.\"},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.claude.json:4:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F10\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The format rule says the qualifier is the one 'the quote writes just before the number'. e1919's quote writes 'followed by an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting particular go[als]'. In the quote, 'up to' appears only before the $3 billion total ('will spend up to $3 billion in Lancium'). 'Up to $1 billion' is the passage's summary wording, not the quote's. So F10's quote_number '$1 billion' with qualifier 'none' matches the quote. Putting the full $1 billion in the low end of the estimate is a judgement inside F4, not a misread of a source figure.\"}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.claude.json:5: ]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:3:    \"v6\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"no\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:5:      \"why\": \"The $679.25–745 billion range follows arithmetically from F7 and F13 and overlaps rung 4. The passages do not support F13’s assumption that 95–100% of the four companies’ total capital expenditure goes to data centers. e5014 says only that “a large portion” goes to AI infrastructure, and e1649 explains Amazon’s spending increase. e1416 and e4082 do not identify the same four companies, while e5376 explicitly covers five. F8 is a reference for total capital expenditure and cannot establish the data-center share.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:6:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:7:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:8:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:9:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:10:      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:12:      \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:13:      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:14:      \"why\": \"F6 says the outside tallies imply “roughly $175-190bn at their centres.” The other three companies’ guidance has a combined midpoint of $555 billion. Subtracting it from the cited tallies gives $177.5 billion, about $190 billion and $205 billion. The last result follows from e2458’s “$760 billion in capex in 2026 alone” and falls outside the claimed range. The error is in the subtraction described in F6’s reasoning. F6’s final $175 billion is a judgment.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:15:    },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:16:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:17:      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:18:      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:19:      \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:20:      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:21:      \"why\": \"The rule says to “Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period” and to look inside stated assumptions for a second dollar value. F6 uses the outside totals “less the other three companies’ guidance” to derive a Microsoft residual. That subtraction uses the tallies as dollar values for the same combined CY2026 capital expenditure that F7 forecasts by adding Microsoft back. The outside figures could serve as comparisons, but here they supply a dollar input to the forecast through F6.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:22:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:23:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:24:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"F1 = $20 billion FY2027 base is quoted in e3660 ('still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027'), and e4856 ('more than double that number in fiscal year 2028', summarised as Vera CPU revenue against the $20 billion FY2027 figure) and e4691 ('Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year') tie that base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue. The stated assumption F7 (85% growth) discounts the management doubling (F2) for time and execution, as the system text requires for a multi-quarter target, and tempers it toward the outside market pace (e4687 '50%'; e4857 '$35 billion to $170 billion'), with adoption breadth (e4689, e5044, e4692) for and AMD's benchmark claims (e5399-e5402, e5404) against. F8 = apply_growth(20, 85) = 20 x 1.85 = 37, which lies in rung 3's band 'at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion'. The forecast follows from the passages and the stated assumption.\"}},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.claude.json:2: \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\", \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"Wrong arithmetic is 'a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs'. F3 = product(F1, F2) = 20 x 2 = 40, and F3 records 40, so it equals its operation within the rule's 0.5%. F2's value of 2 for the quoted 'double' is what the format prescribes ('x: a multiple (double = 2)'; quote_number 'double'), and the quote's 'more than' is recorded where the format puts it, in F2's qualifier ('the qualifier the quote writes just before the number'); the answer's reasons keep the bound ('That gives more than $40 billion (F3)'). That F3 is a lower bound rather than a point, or that the bound leaves $50 billion or more open, is not a mismatch between a computed figure and its inputs, and F3 is only the reference: the outcome F8 does not use it.\"}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"Every link resolves and recomputes within the format's tolerance. F4 = F2 $75.25bn (e109 'the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion') + F3 $89.02bn (e5079 'Data Center alone at $89.02 billion') = 164.27. F9 = apply_growth(F5 $96.22bn, e5078 'Q2 FY2027 revenue landed at $96.22 billion', 16%) = 111.6152, stated 111.62. F11 = 111.62 x 1.15 = 128.363, stated 128.36. F14 = share(111.62, 93) = 103.807, stated 103.8. F15 = share(128.36, 93.5) = 120.0166, stated 120.01, inside 'within 0.5% (at least 0.1)'. F16 = 103.8 + 120.01 = 223.81 (Q3 + Q4 = H2); F17 = 164.27 + 223.81 = 388.08, inside rung 2 ('at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion'). The assumptions are stated estimates with sources. The 16% Q3 pace puts Q3 above the guidance range ('$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%', e2652), and the passages support that beat: e3200 ('actual revenue exceeded that midpoint by $5.2 billion') and e5056 ('revenue could reach roughly $113.6 billion, compared with the current high end of about $110.2 billion'), with supply limits (e5067) given as the reason for a smaller beat than Pradhan's. The 15% Q4 pace rests on e3406 ('growth in its hyperscale business ... should reaccelerate in the fiscal fourth quarter as Vera Rubin supply builds'), and the rising mix on e3399 ('The share rose to 89.8% ... then 92.5%') and on e5223 (growth 'driven primarily by continued strength in the AI data center business'). F9 is the one company Q3 revenue figure on the calculation path. F6 and F7 appear only in F8's rests_on as benchmarks, which the ruling allows. The reference F1 ($361bn, e2295 'we estimate it will be $361 billion in fiscal 2027') is a quoted outside source that also falls in rung 2.\"}},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.claude.json:2: \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.claude.json:3:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F6\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The quote reads 'Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.' The format rule for quote_number says ''$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value.' F6 copies '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%' in full and gives its midpoint, 108, as the value. That is an explicitly permitted form, not a misread. Under the format, 'plus or minus 2%' belongs in quote_number, not in the qualifier field, which is 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after)'. No such word appears, so qualifier 'none' is correct.\"},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.claude.json:4:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\", \"figures\": [\"F15\", \"F11\", \"F13\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"share(F11 128.36, F13 93.5) = 120.0166. The stated 120.01 differs by 0.0066. The format rule says 'The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %)'. A gap of 0.0066 is far inside a 0.1 tolerance, so the figure equals its operation as the format defines equality. No stated rounding convention is required.\"},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.claude.json:5:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 2\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F6\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"e2652 writes '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%'. The quote_number rule permits this expression 'in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value'. F6 gives the midpoint, 108, so the absence of low and high follows the format. 'Plus or minus 2%' is not a qualifier as the format defines one ('the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after)'). The quote has no such word, so 'none' matches the passage. The defect type itself excludes 'what the format rules permit'.\"}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.claude.json:6: ]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The forecast follows from the passages and the stated assumptions. F7 = sum(F1 $195-205bn from e1254 'a range of $195 billion to $205 billion', F2 $125-145bn from e1549 'FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion', F3 $220bn from e1649 'a new projection of $220 billion', F6 $175bn) = $715-745bn, which is correct. F14 = share(F7, F13 95-100%) = 715 x 0.95 = 679.25 up to 745 x 1.00 = 745, also correct. The midpoint is about $712bn. The range overlaps rungs 3 and 4, and the reasons explain the choice of rung 4, which the format allows: 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons'. F6 is a stated estimate with grounds in the passages. A run-rate from e922's '$30.88 billion, up 84%' at about 15% sequential growth gives about $154bn. The outside tallies less the other three companies' guidance give the residual the system text allows: 'a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence'. That residual is $177.5-205bn at the centres, so $175bn is at or below it. F13 follows the format rule 'write the deduction or share as its own figure'. It is a labelled estimate, and the passages back it: the same NVIDIA top-five figure appears as total capex ('CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion', e2759/e5081) and as data-center capex ('will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures', e5376/e4766). e4082 puts 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion', next to the four companies' $720-760bn capex tallies. Caveats: rung 4 needs a share of roughly 96% or more at F7's midpoint, so the share decides the rung. e5014's 'a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure' is weaker than the reasons suggest. The reference F8 is total capex, not data-center spending.\"}},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.claude.json:2: \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.claude.json:3:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\", \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The defect is defined as 'a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs'. Of the figures listed, F1-F3 and F8-F10 are sources and F6 is an estimate ('estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text'). None is computed. The computed figures on the path check out: F7 = 195+125+220+175 to 205+145+220+175 = 715-745, and F14 = 715 x 95/100 = 679.25 to 745 x 100/100 = 745. The reader is right that F6's basis_text 'imply roughly $175-190bn at their centres' leaves out F10's centre (760 - 555 = 205). That is a loose sentence inside an assumption, not a wrong computed figure. It also leans conservative: the correct residual of $177.5-205bn would only support a Microsoft estimate of $175bn or more.\"},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.claude.json:4:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 2\", \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\", \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"F7 is the only forecast figure for the four companies' combined CY2026 capex, and it alone supplies that quantity in the calculation (F14 = share(F7, F13)). F8, F9 and F10 are source figures with basis third_party, and they sit beneath it through F6's rests_on. The format rule allows this: 'Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period ... (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine)'. Inside F6's assumption the tallies serve only as a benchmark for one company's estimate, through the identity the system text allows ('a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it'). The writer then takes $175bn, 'between the two' legs, rather than any tally as the total. This matches the ruling that a figure 'used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the calculation'. The dollar values in the assumption are outside parties' figures, not a second forecast of the total by the writer. Nothing depends on itself: F7 rests on F6, F6 rests on F8-F10, and no figure rests on F7. Reader 2's point that rung 4 depends on the tally leg is a sensitivity of the estimate, not this defect.\"}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.claude.json:5: ]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n --no-heading '.' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q*.{claude,codex}.json\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F7 = F5 x F6 = 17 x 2 = 34, inside rung 3 ($30bn to under $40bn). F5 = the $20bn FY2027 CPU revenue expectation (F1, e3660) x the stated assumption F4 that Vera realizes 85% of it. F6 is the stated assumption that Vera revenue doubles, benchmarked on management's doubling outlook (e4691, e4856 via F2), with AMD's benchmark passages (e5400, e5403) given as the reason not to go above 2x. The band rests on the 85% assumption. No passage gives its size, and the passages it cites (e4689, e5044) speak to breadth of deployment, not a shortfall. Without it the same arithmetic gives management's reference of at least $40bn (F3, rung 2), which the reasons acknowledge.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v29\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e2620 (F1: $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of July 26, 2026, the end of FY2027 Q2) and e2889 (F2: $18 billion committed for the rest of the fiscal year); stated assumption F8 that about $17 billion of the July balance funds before year-end (resting on F1, F2, e4729), so F9 = 25 - 17 = 8; stated assumption F10 that $8 billion of commitments added after July stay unfunded at year-end (resting on F4/e1919, F5/e1957, F6/e4648, F7/e3934, e2207, e2404, e2305, e3455, e3880, e4956); F11 = 8 + 8 = 16, below the $25 billion line of rung 1.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.claude.json:2: \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Base: F1 = $20bn FY2027 CPU revenue guidance (e3660), tied to Vera CPU by e4856 ('more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027'). Forecast: the stated assumption F7 (80-115% FY2028 growth), which discounts management's 'more than double' (F2, e4856) toward the ~50%/yr market pace (F4 e4687; F5-F6 e4857) and AMD's competing benchmark claims (e5399-e5402, e5404), offset by announced adoption (e4689, e5044, e4692). F8 = 20 x (1.80 to 2.15) = $36-43bn, checks out; midpoint $39.5bn and 4 of the 7 points of the range sit in rung 3 ($30-40bn), and the reasons explain the overlap into rung 2. Reference F3 = 20 x 2 = $40bn ('more than' $40bn) is company guidance at the bottom of rung 2, used only as the reference.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\", \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F8\"], \"detail\": \"F7 is a range of 80-115% growth and F8 is $36-43bn (midpoint $39.5bn, as the reasons themselves say). But F7's basis_text ends 'gives 85%', and the reasons say 'I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8)'. So the answer gives a second value for FY2028 Vera CPU revenue: 85% -> $37bn, alongside the F8 range with its $39.5bn midpoint. Both values sit in rung 3, so the chosen rung does not change, but the answer carries two different forecasts of the same quantity and period, and the point figure does not match the figure it cites.\"}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 FY2027 = e111 Q1 $81.62bn + e5078 Q2 $96.22bn = $177.84bn (F3, arithmetic correct). Q3 = $112bn from F2 x (1 + 16.4%), the stated sequential-growth assumption F6, benchmarked between the e2652 guidance midpoint $108bn and the e5056 outside estimate $113.6bn, with the prior-quarter beat e3200 and supply limits e5067 as stated reasons. Q4 = $112bn x 1.17 = $131.04bn from the stated 17% sequential-growth assumption F8 (e2693, e5061, e2759, e5067, e5205). Sum $420.88bn sits in rung 2 [$392bn, $431.2bn). The band is robust: Q3 at guidance with a flat Q4 still gives about $393.8bn, and rung 1 needs Q3+Q4 of at least $253.4bn versus $243.04bn forecast. The stated reference e5204 consensus $411bn is also rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}, \"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 FY2027 Data Center = e109 Q1 $75.25bn + e5079 Q2 $89.02bn = $164.27bn (F14, arithmetic correct). Q3 = 93% (stated share assumption F16, up from the e3399 Q2 share of 92.5%, reasoned from e5061 and e5223) x the $112bn company Q3 forecast = $104.16bn. Q4 = 93.5% (stated share assumption F17) x $131.04bn = $122.52bn. Sum $390.95bn sits in rung 2 [$350bn, $400bn), and the part stays below the whole in Q3, Q4 and the year. Rung 2 holds over plausible ranges (Q3 at guidance and a flat Q4 at a 92.5% share still gives about $364bn); the forecast sits about $9bn below rung 1 and depends on the 17% Q4 company growth assumption. The stated outside reference e2295 Morningstar $361bn (labelled third party, not the writer's estimate) is also rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F15 = F3 + F12 + F14 = 177.82 + 112.86 + 128.66 = 419.34 USD bn, inside rung 2 ($392bn to below $431.2bn). F3 = reported Q1 $81.62bn (e111) + reported Q2 $96.2bn (e2649). F12 = Q3 guidance midpoint $108.0bn (e2652) x 1.045, the stated assumption F11 (4.5% above midpoint) resting on the computed Q2 beat of 5.71% (F7: e3200 $91.0bn midpoint vs $96.2bn), the Q4 FY2026 beat of 4.62% (F10: e2452 $65bn vs $68bn), the beat record in e5054/e5055, trimmed for sold-out capacity and ~70% supply coverage (e4911, e3733). F14 = F12 x 1.14, the stated assumption F13 (14% sequential) benchmarked below Q2's computed 17.86% (F4) and grounded in Q4 hyperscale reacceleration (e3406), Vera Rubin shipments and orders (e2693), supply commitments rising to $279bn (e2759), and supply constraints through FY2028 (e5067, e3733). All arithmetic checks; the rung holds for Q4 sequential growth anywhere between about -10% and +24.5%. Reference F16 = $411bn consensus (e4333), also rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Reported quarters F2 = 81.62 (e111) and F3 = 96.22 (e5078); Q3 estimate F6 = 112.5, stated as a beat on the 108.0 guidance midpoint (F4, e2652) tempered below the repeated-beat-pattern figure 113.6 (F5, e5056), with the prior beat (e3200), Rubin shipments (e2693), CoreWeave's live deployment (e5118) and channel lead times (e5205) as stated grounds; Q4 growth F7 = 12% sequential, stated as close to the Q3 guide's pace on reported Q2 (108/96.22 = 12.2%, used only as a benchmark), with hyperscaler reacceleration (e3406), the AWS commitment (e2686), supply limits (e5067, e5205) and a small H200 allowance (theme v9) as stated grounds; F8 = 112.5 x 1.12 = 126.0; F9 = 81.62 + 96.22 + 112.5 + 126 = 416.34, inside rung 2 ($392bn to below $431.2bn). Reference F1 = $411bn consensus (e5204), also rung 2.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:3:    \"v8\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:5:      \"ground\": \"Reported first half: e109 (Q1 Data Center $75.25bn) + e5079 (Q2 Data Center $89.02bn) = F4 $164.27bn (matches e5309's $164bn). Q3 company revenue F9 = e5078 Q2 $96.22bn x (1 + 16%), the 16% a stated assumption benchmarked between guidance e2652 ($108bn, 12% sequential) and Pradhan's extrapolation e5056 ($113.6bn), with e3200 (Q2 beat), e2693, e5061 and constraint e5067; Q4 company revenue F11 = F9 x 1.15, a stated assumption resting on e3406 (hyperscale reacceleration in Q4), e5118, e5067, e5205. Data Center mix 93% (Q3) and 93.5% (Q4), stated assumptions stepping up from the reported Q2 mix of 92.5% (e3399; F3/F5 = 92.5%) with e5223 naming Data Center as the main growth driver. F14 $103.8bn + F15 $120.01bn = H2 $223.81bn; F17 = $388.08bn, inside rung 2 ($350bn to below $400bn). The band is robust to the assumptions: holding Q3 at guidance with the 92.5% mix (e3401, $99.9bn) and Q4 at analysts' 65% year-over-year growth on Q4 FY2026's $68bn (e5162, e2452) still gives about $368bn, also rung 2; Morningstar's $361bn (e2295) is in rung 2 too.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:9:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:10:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:11:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:12:      \"figures\": [\"F6\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:13:      \"detail\": \"F6 records NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 guidance as 108 with qualifier \\\"none\\\" and no low/high, while its passage e2652 writes \\\"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\\\" (a $105.84bn-$110.16bn range, per e3346). The quote_number keeps the words, but the figure's qualifier differs from the passage. Minor: F6 is only a benchmark in F8's rests_on and is not on the arithmetic path to the outcome, so the forecast and its rung are unaffected.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:14:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:15:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.claude.json:16:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v29\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e2620 (F1: $25 billion of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026) less e2889 (F2: $18 billion committed for the rest of the fiscal year, read as the part of that book funded in FY2027 H2) leaves $7 billion (F3, 25 - 18); plus the writer's stated estimate F4 of $2-13 billion of post-quarter commitments still unfunded at year end, built from e1970 (SB Energy second $1.5 billion tranche), e1919 (Lancium milestone $1 billion), e4648 (Anthropic anchor up to $10 billion), with funded-at-closing deals e2207, e3474, e1957 left out and e2643 / e4487 arguing against further large staged commitments; F5 = $9-20 billion (7+2, 7+13), wholly below the $25 billion boundary, so rung 1. The distinction from Bank of America's $70 billion (e1732, funded plus unfunded, consistent with e4729's >$42 billion bought plus $25 billion owed) is stated and checkable.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"F1 ($20bn FY2027 base, e3660, tied to Vera by e4856) and the stated growth assumption F7 (80-115%, resting on F2/e4856 'more than double', F4/e4687, F5-F6/e4857, e4689, e5044, e4692, e5399-e5402, e5404); F8 = apply_growth(F1, F7) = $36-43bn. Reference F3 = 20 x 2 = $40bn (e3660, e4856).\", \"why_not\": \"The chosen rung 4 (Bearish, $20bn to below $30bn) does not hold or overlap the outcome F8 ($36-43bn), which lies in rungs 3 and 2; the reasons themselves contradict the answer, opening with 'I choose rung 4' and closing with '$37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral)'. In addition, F7's stated assumption arrives at a single 85% ('gives 85%') and says the 'more than double' target is discounted rather than taken in full, yet the figure carries a range of 80-115% whose upper end exceeds that undiscounted 100% benchmark; the range is not derived from anything stated.\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"broken structure\", \"figures\": [\"F8\"], \"detail\": \"Rung 4's band ($20bn to below $30bn) does not reach the outcome range $36-43bn (F8 = 20 x 1.80 to 20 x 2.15); the range overlaps rung 3 ($30-40bn) and rung 2 ($40-50bn) only. The reasons also name rung 3 for the $37bn point while the answer records rung 4.\"}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F14 = F7 x F13. F7 ($715-745bn) is the sum of Alphabet guidance F1 $195-205bn (e1254), Meta guidance F2 $125-145bn (e1549), Amazon projection F3 $220bn (e1649) and the stated Microsoft estimate F6 $175bn (from e922's $30.88bn quarter up 84%, e4405's backlog, and the outside tallies). F13 is a stated 95-100% data-center share, reasoned from outlets that describe the same totals as data-center spending (e1416, e4082, e5376). The result is $679.25-745bn, midpoint about $712bn. The range overlaps rungs 3 and 4, and the reasons explain the choice of rung 4. The independent tallies of total capital expenditure, e5014 $720-745bn, e1856 ~$745bn and e2458 $760bn, put a 95-100% share in rung 4 as well. Caveats: the reference F8 (e5014) is total capital expenditure, not data-center spending. e5014's 'a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure' is weaker support for 95% than the reasons suggest. The reasons call e2759 a data-center figure, but it is total CapEx for the top five.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.claude.json:2: \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\", \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"], \"detail\": \"The four companies' combined CY2026 capital expenditure gets two different values on the outcome's path. F7 computes it as $715-745bn. Inside F6's stated assumption, the outside tallies F8 ($720-745bn), F9 ($745bn) and F10 ($760bn) are used as the dollar value of that same quantity: tally minus the other three companies' $540-570bn is used to back out Microsoft ('roughly $175-190bn at their centres'; F10's centre would actually give about $205bn). These tallies are not a rate or a benchmark for some other quantity. They are values of the very total F7 computes, so the total partly derives from itself and the build is not independent of the reference F8. The circular leg decides the band. With only F6's run-rate leg (Microsoft about $155bn), the total is $695-725bn and the data-center spending at 95-100% is $660-725bn, midpoint about $693bn, which is in rung 3.\"}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Reported H1 Data Center revenue $164.25bn (F3 = e109 $75.25bn + e2650 $89.0bn; matches e5309 $164bn) plus H2 Data Center $223.1bn (F17), giving $387.35bn (F18), inside rung 2 ($350bn to below $400bn). H2 rests on: Q3 company revenue = e2652 guidance $108.0bn x (1 + stated 5% beat assumption F11, grounded in F6 5.71% Q2 beat [e3200, e2649], F9 4.62% Q4 FY2026 beat [e2452], e5054/e5055 3.1% above the high end = about 5% above midpoint, capped by e4911) = $113.4bn (F12); Q4 = F12 x 1.12 = $127.01bn (F14) on the stated 12% sequential assumption (F13) benchmarked on the guidance-implied 12% (e5005), with e3406/e5061/e4283 upside and e3733/e5067 supply limits; H2 company $240.41bn x stated Data Center share 92.8% (F16; Q2 share 89.0/96.2 = 92.5%, rising each quarter per e3399, e5223). All arithmetic checks. The band is robust: even Q3 guidance x 92.5% (about $99.9bn, e3401) held flat for Q4 gives about $364bn, still rung 2. Reference: Morningstar $361bn (F19, e2295), a third-party estimate, also rung 2. Note: the reasons call the $185.8bn rung-3 boundary 'below the Q2 pace', but $185.8bn over two quarters (about $92.9bn a quarter) is above the Q2 pace of $89bn; this misstates the margin but does not change the band.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e3660 gives the FY2027 base of $20 billion (F1); e4856 and e4691 tie that base and the FY2028 'more than double' to Vera CPU revenue (F2, giving the reference F3 = 20 x 2 = $40 billion, rung 2's bottom edge). The forecast rests on the stated assumption F7: 85% growth, the management doubling discounted for time and execution and tempered toward the ~50% market pace (e4687 via F4; e4857 via F5/F6), with adoption breadth (e4689, e5044, e4692) for and AMD's benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) against. F8 = 20 x 1.85 = $37 billion, inside rung 3's $30-40 billion band.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F13 = F11 x F12 = 745 x 90% = 670.5, inside rung 3 (600 to below 700). F11 (745 total capex) is a stated assumption: the top of Zacks's roughly 720-745 range (e5014), below the 760 plan (e2458). The company guidance in view backs it (Alphabet 195-205 e1254, Amazon 220 e1649, Meta 125-145 e1549, which leaves about 175-205 for Microsoft), and the slippage reasons cite e122 and e5292. F12 (90% data-center share) is the writer's own stated assumption, and the answer says the packet has no group-wide split. Its only grounds are Meta's 130 AI capex (e3686) against its 125-145 guidance (e1549), a ratio of about 90-104%, and Zacks's 'large portion' wording (e5014). The rung depends on this assumption: at a share of about 94% or more, the same 745 total lands in rung 4. The answer names the spending mix as its biggest uncertainty near that cutoff.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:3:    \"v8\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"e109 and e5079 establish $164.27 billion in first-half Data Center revenue. The stated assumptions apply 16% and 15% sequential company revenue growth to the $96.22 billion Q2 base in e5078, then use Data Center shares of 93% and 93.5%. The assumptions draw on guidance and the prior beat (e2652, e3200, e5056), the Rubin ramp and supply constraints (e2693, e5061, e3406, e5118, e5067, e5205), and the rising Data Center share (e3399, e5223). They produce about $388 billion, within rung 2's $350 billion to under $400 billion band. Morningstar's separate $361 billion estimate is directly sourced to e2295.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:10:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:12:      \"figures\": [\"F6\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:13:      \"detail\": \"e2652 gives $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%. F6 preserves that wording in the quote but records the guidance as an unqualified point with no bounds.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:14:    },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:15:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:16:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:17:      \"figures\": [\"F15\", \"F11\", \"F13\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:18:      \"detail\": \"The listed inputs give 128.36 × 93.5% = 120.0166, which rounds to 120.02 billion. The stated 120.01 billion can be reproduced by carrying unrounded upstream values, but the answer does not state that convention. The difference leaves the forecast in rung 2.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:19:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:20:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json:21:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:3:    \"v4\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 revenue totals $177.84 billion (e111, e5078). The stated 16.4% sequential growth assumption gives $112 billion for Q3, between the $108 billion guidance midpoint and the roughly $113.6 billion outside estimate (e2652, e3200, e5056). The stated 17% Q4 growth assumption gives $131.04 billion, drawing on Vera Rubin production and stronger hyperscaler demand, with supply constraints limiting upside (e2693, e5061, e5138, e5067, e5205). The sum is $420.88 billion, in rung 2.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:6:    },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:7:    \"v8\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:8:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:9:      \"ground\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue totals $164.27 billion (e109, e5079). Starting from the 92.5% Q2 share (e3399), the answer assumes Data Center reaches 93% of company revenue in Q3 and 93.5% in Q4 as AI cloud, industrial and enterprise sales grow and Vera Rubin supply expands (e5223, e5061, e5138). Those shares give $104.16 billion and $122.52 billion using the same company forecasts as v4. The sum is $390.95 billion, in rung 2.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:10:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:11:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:12:  \"defects\": []\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q01.codex.json:13:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v4\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 reported: F1 81.62 (e111) + F2 96.2 (e2649) = F3 177.82 (matches e4992's $177.8bn). Q3: F9 112.5, a stated estimate resting on the Q3 guide F7 105.84-110.16 (e2652, 108.0 +/-2%), the beat record (e5054 12 straight beats of the high end; e5055/F8 3.1% average, i.e. 110.16 x 1.031 = 113.6; e3200 Q2 beat of $5.2bn; e2452 Q4 FY26 beat of $3bn), trimmed for supply caps (e3115, e4911). Q4: F11 12% sequential, benchmarked on the guided Q3 pace F10 (e4899), supported by hyperscale reacceleration in Q4 (e3406, e4283), commitments rising $119bn to $279bn (e2759) and the ~70% FY2028 supply-capped outlook (e2691; ~14% quarterly growth from a 126 exit checks out), and held below the actual 17-18% pace because of e5067. F12 = 112.5 x 1.12 = 126.0; F13 = 238.5; F14 = 177.82 + 238.5 = 416.32, inside rung 2 [392, 431.2). The guided Q3 growth rate F10 is only a benchmark for F11; F9 alone supplies Q3 in the calculation. Reference F19 $411bn consensus (e4333) is also rung 2, cross-checked by 87.4-88% consensus growth on $215.9bn (e4990, e5129, e4273) giving ~$405bn. Sensitivity (Q3 at 108, Q4 +5%) gives 399.2, still rung 2; the analysts' quarterly path (e5161, e5162 on 57.01 and 68) gives ~398.3, also rung 2. Reading the '70% full-year' reports (e3280, e3890, e4628) as the FY2028 outlook is supported by the transcript e2691 and by the arithmetic: 367 would need Q4 of ~81, below Q1. Small wording slips that do not move the band: F11's text puts actual sequential growth at '17-20%', but Q1 to Q2 is 17.9% and Q2 to F9 is 16.9%; and the rung-1 example 'Q4 near $138bn (+20%)' assumes a Q3 above F9 (at F9's 112.5, Q4 would need ~$141bn, +25%).\", \"why_not\": \"\"}, \"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"H1 reported: F4 75.25 (e109) + F5 89.0 (e2650) = F6 164.25 (matches e5309's $164bn). H2 share: F16 93.0%, a stated estimate from the rising Data Center share (e3399/F15: 87.9, 89.8, 91.5, 92.2, 92.5%, with the gains narrowing), Q3 growth said to come mainly from AI data center (e5223, e4283, e2721), and the non-Data Center remainder, Edge Computing $7.2bn (= 96.2 - 89.0), growing a slower 13% (e5059). F17 = F13 238.5 x 0.93 = 221.81; F18 = 164.25 + 221.81 = 386.06, inside rung 2 [350, 400), and below the whole's 416.32 (H2 Data Center is 93% of the same H2 company figure, so no part exceeds its whole and no part is added into the whole). Reference F20: Morningstar's $361bn (e2295), a third party's estimate labelled third_party and not the writer's own, also rung 2. The answer explicitly declines to apply a quarter's mix to the $411bn consensus as a reference. Sensitivity (H2 at the guide midpoint, Q4 +5%, 92.5% share) gives 369.05, still rung 2; rung 1 needs 235.75 of H2 Data Center (~253.5 of H2 company); rung 3 needs H2 Data Center below 185.75, which is under even the Q3 low end carried flat at 92.5% (~195.8). Rejecting e4907's $83.7bn against the company's $89.0bn release (e2650) is justified.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:3:    \"v29\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"no\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"The answer starts with the $25 billion July 26 balance in e2620, subtracts the $18 billion discussed in e2889, and adds an estimated $2–13 billion of new commitments still unfunded at year-end. That gives $9–20 billion, within rung 1.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"e2889 does not establish that the $18 billion is part of the July 26 balance or that NVIDIA will fund all of it by year-end. The answer treats both points as a company-provided payment schedule. Without that deduction, the forecast has not established a year-end balance below $25 billion.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:10:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:12:      \"figures\": [\"F2\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:13:      \"detail\": \"e2889 reports $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of the fiscal year. F2 presents it as guidance for payments that will reduce the July 26 balance, which the passage does not specify.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:14:    },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:15:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:16:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:17:      \"figures\": [\"F10\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:18:      \"detail\": \"e1919 describes the milestone-linked Lancium tranche as up to $1 billion. F10 drops the upper-limit qualifier, and the low-end estimate uses the full amount.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:19:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:20:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q09.codex.json:21:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.codex.json:3:    \"v41\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"The answer assumes Vera realizes 85% of NVIDIA's $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook (e3660), yielding $17 billion. It uses management's growth outlook (e4691, e4856) as a benchmark and assumes that $17 billion doubles to $34 billion in FY2028, within rung 3 ($30 billion to below $40 billion). Broad deployment plans and Anthropic's evaluation support the ramp (e4689, e5044), while AMD's benchmarks point to competition (e5400, e5403).\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": []\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q02.codex.json:10:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:3:    \"v6\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"The answer uses $745 billion in total capex, the high end of e5014, and assumes 90% goes to data centers based on e5014 and the Meta comparison in e1549 and e3686. That yields $670.5 billion, within rung 3's $600 billion to under $700 billion band.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": []\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:10:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:3:    \"v6\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"no\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"e1254, e1549 and e1649 supply three companies' total-capex guidance. F6 assumes $175 billion for Microsoft using e922, e4405, e5014, e1856 and e2458. F13 assumes 95–100% of the resulting total goes to data centers, giving F14 a range of $679.25–745 billion and a midpoint in rung 4.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"The arithmetic from the assumed share to rung 4 is checkable, but the passages do not substantiate the 95–100% share. e5014 gives total capex and says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure. e1649 describes what drove Amazon's increase. e1416 and e4082 do not identify the same four companies, and e5376 covers five. The $720–745 billion reference is for total capex, so it does not independently ground the data-center forecast.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:10:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:12:      \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:13:      \"detail\": \"The other three companies' guidance has a combined midpoint of $555 billion. Subtracting that from the cited tallies gives $177.5 billion from F8's midpoint, $190 billion from F9 and $205 billion from F10. The claimed $175–190 billion residual omits the $205 billion implied by the included $760 billion tally.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:14:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:15:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json:16:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.codex.json:3:    \"v8\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"Reported first-half Data Center revenue is $164.25 billion (e109, e2650). The answer assumes a 5% beat on the $108 billion Q3 company guidance midpoint, based on prior beats (e2652, e2649, e3200, e2452, e5054, e5055), yielding $113.4 billion. It uses the 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance as a benchmark for Q4, balancing the Vera Rubin ramp against supply constraints (e5005, e3406, e2759, e3733, e5067), yielding $127.01 billion. Applying the stated 92.8% Data Center share assumption, supported by the recent mix trend (e3399, e5223), gives $223.10 billion in second-half Data Center revenue and $387.35 billion for FY2027. That falls in rung 2 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). The $361 billion reference is Morningstar's published estimate (e2295).\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": []\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q05.codex.json:10:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:3:    \"v41\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"no\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"The $20 billion FY2027 base (e3660, e4856) and the stated 80% to 115% growth assumption yield $36 billion to $43 billion. The assumption weighs NVIDIA's doubling outlook (e4691, e4856), market growth (e4687, e4857), adoption (e4689, e5044, e4692), and AMD's competitive benchmarks (e5399–e5402, e5404).\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"The forecast range overlaps rungs 3 and 2. The selected rung 4 requires at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. The $37 billion point estimate in the reasons also falls in rung 3.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:10:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"broken structure\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:12:      \"figures\": [\"F8\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:13:      \"detail\": \"Rung 4 does not overlap F8's $36 billion to $43 billion range. The reasons also identify rung 3 for the $37 billion point estimate.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:14:    },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:15:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:16:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:17:      \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:18:      \"detail\": \"Multiplying $20 billion by more than 2 gives a strict lower bound of more than $40 billion. F3 records exactly $40 billion, although the reasons state the bound correctly.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:19:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:20:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex.json:21:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.codex.json:3:    \"v29\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"e2620 reports $25 billion outstanding at July 26. The answer assumes the $18 billion cited in e2889 is part of that balance scheduled to fund by year-end, and estimates that $17 billion will actually fund, leaving $8 billion. It estimates another $8 billion in new commitments will remain unfunded, drawing on the SB Energy, Lancium and Poolside deals (e1957, e1919, e2404, e2207) and discounting possible lab deals for uncertain signing and funding dates (e4648, e3934). The resulting $16 billion falls below the $25 billion cutoff for rung 1.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": []\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q03.codex.json:10:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:3:    \"v41\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"The $20 billion FY2027 base in e3660 and e4856 and management's doubling benchmark in e4691 and e4856 anchor the forecast. The writer assumes 85% growth after weighing the slower market growth in e4687 and e4857, Vera adoption in e4689 and e5044, and AMD's competitive benchmarks in e5399–e5404. That gives $37 billion, within rung 3. The stated $36–43 billion range also overlaps rung 3 and has a midpoint of $39.5 billion.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:6:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:7:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:8:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:9:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:10:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:11:      \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:12:      \"detail\": \"F2 is strictly greater than 2×. Multiplying it by $20 billion gives strictly more than $40 billion. F3 records $40 billion as an exact value, although the prose preserves the lower bound.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:13:    },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:14:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:15:      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:16:      \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F8\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:17:      \"detail\": \"The answer first uses the $36–43 billion range as the FY2028 forecast, then separately calls $37 billion the forecast and attributes that point to F8. F8 contains the range only. Both presentations support rung 3.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:18:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:19:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex.json:20:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:3:    \"v4\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 revenue totals $177.82 billion (e111, e2649). The writer assumes $112.5 billion for Q3 by applying a smaller-than-usual beat to guidance given supply constraints (e2652, e5054, e5055, e3115, e4911). For Q4, the writer assumes 12% sequential growth, using the Q3 guidance rate as a benchmark and citing the expected hyperscale pickup (e4899, e4283, e2759, e5067). That gives $126 billion for Q4 and $416.32 billion for the year, within rung 2.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:7:    },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:8:    \"v8\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:9:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:10:      \"ground\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue totals $164.25 billion (e109, e2650). The writer assumes Data Center accounts for 93% of the shared $238.5 billion second-half company forecast, up from 92.5% in Q2 because the share has been rising and management expects Data Center to drive Q3 growth (e3399, e5223, e4283, e5059). That gives $386.06 billion for the year after rounding, within rung 2.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:11:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:12:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:13:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:14:  \"defects\": []\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q13.codex.json:15:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.codex.json:3:    \"v4\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 revenue total $177.82 billion (e111, e2649). A stated 4.5% beat of the $108 billion Q3 guidance midpoint gives $112.86 billion, consistent with past guidance beats and tempered by supply limits (e2652, e3200, e2452, e5054, e5055, e4911, e3733). The stated 14% Q4 growth assumption reflects the Vera Rubin ramp and continued supply constraints (e3406, e2693, e2759, e5067), giving $128.66 billion. The full-year total of $419.34 billion falls in rung 2. The $411 billion market reference comes directly from e4333.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": []\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q11.codex.json:10:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:3:    \"v41\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"The $20 billion FY2027 base comes from e3660 and e4856. The answer assumes 85% FY2028 growth, discounting management's doubling benchmark (e4691, e4856) for execution risk and AMD competition (e5399–e5402, e5404), while weighing Vera adoption (e4689, e5044) and CPU market growth (e4687, e4857). $20 billion × 1.85 = $37 billion, inside rung 3's $30 billion to under $40 billion band.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": [\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:10:    {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:12:      \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\"],\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:13:      \"detail\": \"F2 says more than 2×, so applying it to $20 billion gives a strict lower bound above $40 billion. The prose preserves that bound, but F3 records exactly $40 billion. The bound alone also leaves open revenue of $50 billion or more.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:14:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:15:  ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json:16:}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.codex.json:1:{\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.codex.json:2:  \"members\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.codex.json:3:    \"v4\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"Reported Q1 and Q2 revenue was $81.62 billion and $96.22 billion (e111, e5078). The writer estimates $112.5 billion for Q3, above the $108 billion guidance midpoint given the prior beat and Rubin shipments, but below the roughly $113.6 billion estimate that assumes the recent beat pattern repeats (e2652, e3200, e2693, e5118, e5056, e5205). For Q4, the writer assumes 12% growth on the $112.5 billion Q3 estimate, using the pace implied by Q3 guidance as a benchmark and weighing the expected pickup in hyperscaler sales against supply constraints (e3406, e2686, e5067, e5205). That gives $126 billion for Q4 and $416.34 billion for FY2027, within rung 2. The separate $411 billion market reference comes from analyst consensus (e5204).\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.codex.json:7:    }\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.codex.json:8:  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.codex.json:9:  \"defects\": []\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q04.codex.json:10:}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n --no-heading '\"'^#|''^[-] Reader|''^.*plus or minus 2%|''^.*85%.*37 billion'\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q*.md poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q{06,10,12,14}.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n --no-heading '\"'^#|''^[-] Reader|''^.*plus or minus 2%|''^.*85%.*37 billion'\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q*.md poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q{06,10,12,14}.md\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.md:1:# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, SMALL-DESIGN.md §5)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.md:33:# The request as the writer saw it\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.md:96:# The answer\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.md:98:{\"figures\": [{\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 20, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F2\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth multiple\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 2, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"x\", \"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"quote_number\": \"double\", \"qualifier\": \"more than\", \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F3\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 40, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"], \"operation\": \"product\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": [\"v41\"]}, {\"id\": \"F4\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"server CPU market\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"server CPU\", \"period\": \"annual rate through CY2030\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 50, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\", \"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"quote_number\": \"50%\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F5\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"CPU market\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 35, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"quote_number\": \"$35 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F6\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"CPU market\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"CY2030\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 170, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"quote_number\": \"$170 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F7\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": null, \"low\": 80.0, \"high\": 115.0, \"unit\": \"%\", \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth on the FY2027 base. Management's 'more than double' (F2, at least 100% growth) is the benchmark, but it is a target for a fiscal year that has not started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full. Outside views put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD, F4; Bank of America's fivefold path from F5 to F6), so a doubling needs large share gains. Broad announced adoption (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689; Anthropic evaluating Vera, e5044; Arm servers' large revenue share, e4692) supports a pace well above the market, while AMD's own benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) show standalone CPU share will be contested. Weighting the company target most heavily, tempered toward the market pace, gives 85%.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F2\", \"F4\", \"F5\", \"F6\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e4692\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5404\"], \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F8\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": null, \"low\": 36.0, \"high\": 43.0, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F7\"], \"operation\": \"apply_growth\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [\"v41\"], \"reference_for\": []}], \"answers\": [{\"view_id\": \"v41\", \"shape\": \"forecast\", \"rung\": 3, \"reference_unavailable\": null, \"reasons\": \"The growth estimate is a range, so the outcome runs from about $36 billion to $43 billion. Its midpoint, near $39.5 billion, and most of the range lie in rung 3, so I choose rung 3; only the top of the range reaches rung 2. Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\", \"why\": null, \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4691\", \"e4764\", \"e4856\", \"e4687\", \"e4857\", \"e4689\", \"e4692\", \"e5044\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5404\"], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5044\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5399\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5400\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5401\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5402\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5403\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5404\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}], \"theme_pushes\": [{\"view_id\": \"v7\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"none\"}]}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.md:1:# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, SMALL-DESIGN.md §5)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.md:33:# The request as the writer saw it\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.md:96:# The answer\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.md:98:{\"figures\": [{\"id\": \"F1\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2027\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 20, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F2\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth multiple\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 2, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"x\", \"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"quote_number\": \"double\", \"qualifier\": \"more than\", \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F3\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 40, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"], \"operation\": \"product\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": [\"v41\"]}, {\"id\": \"F4\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"server CPU market\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"server CPU\", \"period\": \"annual rate through CY2030\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 50, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"%\", \"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"quote_number\": \"50%\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F5\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"CPU market\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 35, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"quote_number\": \"$35 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F6\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"CPU market\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"CPU\", \"period\": \"CY2030\"}, \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"third_party\", \"value\": 170, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"quote_number\": \"$170 billion\", \"qualifier\": \"none\", \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F7\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue growth\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"estimate\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": null, \"low\": 80.0, \"high\": 115.0, \"unit\": \"%\", \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth on the FY2027 base. Management's 'more than double' (F2, at least 100% growth) is the benchmark, but it is a target for a fiscal year that has not started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full. Outside views put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD, F4; Bank of America's fivefold path from F5 to F6), so a doubling needs large share gains. Broad announced adoption (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689; Anthropic evaluating Vera, e5044; Arm servers' large revenue share, e4692) supports a pace well above the market, while AMD's own benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) show standalone CPU share will be contested. Weighting the company target most heavily, tempered toward the market pace, gives 85%.\", \"rests_on\": [\"F2\", \"F4\", \"F5\", \"F6\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e4692\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5404\"], \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []}, {\"id\": \"F8\", \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\", \"period\": \"FY2028\"}, \"kind\": \"computed\", \"basis\": \"forecast\", \"value\": null, \"low\": 36.0, \"high\": 43.0, \"unit\": \"USD bn\", \"evidence_id\": null, \"quote_number\": null, \"qualifier\": null, \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F7\"], \"operation\": \"apply_growth\", \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null, \"outcome_for\": [\"v41\"], \"reference_for\": []}], \"answers\": [{\"view_id\": \"v41\", \"shape\": \"forecast\", \"rung\": 4, \"reference_unavailable\": null, \"reasons\": \"The growth estimate is a range, so the outcome runs from about $36 billion to $43 billion. I choose rung 4. Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\", \"why\": null, \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4691\", \"e4764\", \"e4856\", \"e4687\", \"e4857\", \"e4689\", \"e4692\", \"e5044\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5404\"], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5044\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5399\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5400\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5401\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5402\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5403\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e5404\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}], \"theme_pushes\": [{\"view_id\": \"v7\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"none\"}]}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.md:1:# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, SMALL-DESIGN.md §5)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.md:33:# The request as the writer saw it\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.md:96:# The answer\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.md:259:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:1:# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:29:# The two readings\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:30:- Reader 1 on member v41: supported = yes. Ground: The $20 billion FY2027 base comes from e3660 and e4856. The answer assumes 85% FY2028 growth, discounting management's doubling benchmark (e4691, e4856) for execution risk and AMD competition (e5399–e5402, e5404), while weighing Vera adoption (e4689, e5044) and CPU market growth (e4687, e4857). $20 billion × 1.85 = $37 billion, inside rung 3's $30 billion to under $40 billion band.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:31:- Reader 1 marks a defect: wrong arithmetic (figures ['F1', 'F2', 'F3']): F2 says more than 2×, so applying it to $20 billion gives a strict lower bound above $40 billion. The prose preserves that bound, but F3 records exactly $40 billion. The bound alone also leaves open revenue of $50 billion or more.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:32:- Reader 2 on member v41: supported = yes. Ground: e3660 gives the FY2027 base of $20 billion (F1); e4856 and e4691 tie that base and the FY2028 'more than double' to Vera CPU revenue (F2, giving the reference F3 = 20 x 2 = $40 billion, rung 2's bottom edge). The forecast rests on the stated assumption F7: 85% growth, the management doubling discounted for time and execution and tempered toward the ~50% market pace (e4687 via F4; e4857 via F5/F6), with adoption breadth (e4689, e5044, e4692) for and AMD's benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) against. F8 = 20 x 1.85 = $37 billion, inside rung 3's $30-40 billion band.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:33:- Reader 2 marks no defect.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:35:# The system text the writer was given\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:47:# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:75:# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:137:# The answer\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.md:299:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:1:# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:29:# The two readings\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:30:- Reader 1 on member v29: supported = no. Ground: The answer starts with the $25 billion July 26 balance in e2620, subtracts the $18 billion discussed in e2889, and adds an estimated $2–13 billion of new commitments still unfunded at year-end. That gives $9–20 billion, within rung 1. Why not: e2889 does not establish that the $18 billion is part of the July 26 balance or that NVIDIA will fund all of it by year-end. The answer treats both points as a company-provided payment schedule. Without that deduction, the forecast has not established a year-end balance below $25 billion.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:31:- Reader 1 marks a defect: number misread (figures ['F2']): e2889 reports $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of the fiscal year. F2 presents it as guidance for payments that will reduce the July 26 balance, which the passage does not specify.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:32:- Reader 1 marks a defect: number misread (figures ['F10']): e1919 describes the milestone-linked Lancium tranche as up to $1 billion. F10 drops the upper-limit qualifier, and the low-end estimate uses the full amount.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:33:- Reader 2 on member v29: supported = yes. Ground: e2620 (F1: $25 billion of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026) less e2889 (F2: $18 billion committed for the rest of the fiscal year, read as the part of that book funded in FY2027 H2) leaves $7 billion (F3, 25 - 18); plus the writer's stated estimate F4 of $2-13 billion of post-quarter commitments still unfunded at year end, built from e1970 (SB Energy second $1.5 billion tranche), e1919 (Lancium milestone $1 billion), e4648 (Anthropic anchor up to $10 billion), with funded-at-closing deals e2207, e3474, e1957 left out and e2643 / e4487 arguing against further large staged commitments; F5 = $9-20 billion (7+2, 7+13), wholly below the $25 billion boundary, so rung 1. The distinction from Bank of America's $70 billion (e1732, funded plus unfunded, consistent with e4729's >$42 billion bought plus $25 billion owed) is stated and checkable.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:34:- Reader 2 marks no defect.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:36:# The system text the writer was given\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:48:# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:76:# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.md:176:# The answer\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.md:1:# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.md:29:# The two readings\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.md:30:- Reader 1 on member v6: supported = no. Ground: e1254, e1549 and e1649 supply three companies' total-capex guidance. F6 assumes $175 billion for Microsoft using e922, e4405, e5014, e1856 and e2458. F13 assumes 95–100% of the resulting total goes to data centers, giving F14 a range of $679.25–745 billion and a midpoint in rung 4. Why not: The arithmetic from the assumed share to rung 4 is checkable, but the passages do not substantiate the 95–100% share. e5014 gives total capex and says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure. e1649 describes what drove Amazon's increase. e1416 and e4082 do not identify the same four companies, and e5376 covers five. The $720–745 billion reference is for total capex, so it does not independently ground the data-center forecast.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.md:31:- Reader 1 marks a defect: wrong arithmetic (figures ['F1', 'F2', 'F3', 'F6', 'F8', 'F9', 'F10']): The other three companies' guidance has a combined midpoint of $555 billion. Subtracting that from the cited tallies gives $177.5 billion from F8's midpoint, $190 billion from F9 and $205 billion from F10. The claimed $175–190 billion residual omits the $205 billion implied by the included $760 billion tally.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.md:32:- Reader 2 on member v6: supported = yes. Ground: F14 = F7 x F13. F7 ($715-745bn) is the sum of Alphabet guidance F1 $195-205bn (e1254), Meta guidance F2 $125-145bn (e1549), Amazon projection F3 $220bn (e1649) and the stated Microsoft estimate F6 $175bn (from e922's $30.88bn quarter up 84%, e4405's backlog, and the outside tallies). F13 is a stated 95-100% data-center share, reasoned from outlets that describe the same totals as data-center spending (e1416, e4082, e5376). The result is $679.25-745bn, midpoint about $712bn. The range overlaps rungs 3 and 4, and the reasons explain the choice of rung 4. The independent tallies of total capital expenditure, e5014 $720-745bn, e1856 ~$745bn and e2458 $760bn, put a 95-100% share in rung 4 as well. Caveats: the reference F8 (e5014) is total capital expenditure, not data-center spending. e5014's 'a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure' is weaker support for 95% than the reasons suggest. The reasons call e2759 a data-center figure, but it is total CapEx for the top five.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.md:33:- Reader 2 marks a defect: two forecasts of one quarter (figures ['F7', 'F6', 'F8', 'F9', 'F10']): The four companies' combined CY2026 capital expenditure gets two different values on the outcome's path. F7 computes it as $715-745bn. Inside F6's stated assumption, the outside tallies F8 ($720-745bn), F9 ($745bn) and F10 ($760bn) are used as the dollar value of that same quantity: tally minus the other three companies' $540-570bn is used to back out Microsoft ('roughly $175-190bn at their centres'; F10's centre would actually give about $205bn). These tallies are not a rate or a benchmark for some other quantity. They are values of the very total F7 computes, so the total partly derives from itself and the build is not independent of the reference F8. The circular leg decides the band. With only F6's run-rate leg (Microsoft about $155bn), the total is $695-725bn and the data-center spending at 95-100% is $660-725bn, midpoint about $693bn, which is in rung 3.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.md:35:# The system text the writer was given\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.md:47:# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.md:75:# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.md:211:# The answer\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:1:# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, SMALL-DESIGN.md §5)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:33:# The request as the writer saw it\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:62:- e2652 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, with no Data Center compute revenue from China assumed.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:63:    Quote: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:149:- e4899 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:13952dff579190ce77353acd 2026-09-14] NVIDIA guided to approximately $108 billion in total revenue, plus or minus 2%, for FY2027 Q3, implying 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:150:    Quote: Looking ahead, NVIDIA expects revenues to reach approximately $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, representing 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:196:- e4963 [party_statement; Stephen Guilfoyle; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2fc71ee9730f9420d6d130bd 2026-09-14] NVIDIA projected current-quarter revenue of $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, versus consensus of $104.6 billion; the projection implied roughly 90% year-over-year growth.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:238:- e5005 [party_statement; Zacks analyst; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:955c64e5a5907651550e94b8 2026-09-15] NVIDIA guided fiscal Q3 2027 total revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, 12% above the prior quarter from the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:239:    Quote: The company expects revenues of around $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially from the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:283:- e5052 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] NVIDIA guided for Q3 FY2027 revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2% ($105.8 billion to $110.2 billion).\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:284:    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) expects third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:334:- e5081 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management guided Q3 FY2027 total revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:335:    Quote: Management then guided Q3 to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:382:- e5111 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA management 2026-08-26; from gnews:bc6926bf31d3a4d8006dce46 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management forecast fiscal Q3 2027 revenue of about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, up 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:383:    Quote: Management projects revenues of around $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:492:- e5223 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:12a943c49b0c41542e6110b5 2026-09-18] NVIDIA guided next-quarter total revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, citing continued strength in its AI Data Center business as the primary growth driver.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:493:    Quote: First, it guided total revenue for the next quarter to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and said growth would be driven primarily by continued strength in the AI data center business.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:597:- e5300 [party_statement; benzinga.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbf072e65acf3391f77465fd 2026-09-18] NVIDIA forecast third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:598:    Quote: the company forecast third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:610:- e5314 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:57a69374b8fa083ce36ecfb1 2026-09-18] NVIDIA guided FY2027 Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, about 12% above FY2027 Q2 revenue at the guidance midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:611:    Quote: Looking ahead, NVIDIA expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues to reach $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially from the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:635:- e2720 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 total revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:636:    Quote: Total revenue is expected to be $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:808:# The answer\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md:856:      \"evidence_id\": \"e2652\", \"quote_number\": \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\", \"qualifier\": \"none\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:1:# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:29:# The two readings\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:30:- Reader 1 on member v8: supported = yes. Ground: e109 and e5079 establish $164.27 billion in first-half Data Center revenue. The stated assumptions apply 16% and 15% sequential company revenue growth to the $96.22 billion Q2 base in e5078, then use Data Center shares of 93% and 93.5%. The assumptions draw on guidance and the prior beat (e2652, e3200, e5056), the Rubin ramp and supply constraints (e2693, e5061, e3406, e5118, e5067, e5205), and the rising Data Center share (e3399, e5223). They produce about $388 billion, within rung 2's $350 billion to under $400 billion band. Morningstar's separate $361 billion estimate is directly sourced to e2295.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:31:- Reader 1 marks a defect: number misread (figures ['F6']): e2652 gives $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%. F6 preserves that wording in the quote but records the guidance as an unqualified point with no bounds.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:32:- Reader 1 marks a defect: wrong arithmetic (figures ['F15', 'F11', 'F13']): The listed inputs give 128.36 × 93.5% = 120.0166, which rounds to 120.02 billion. The stated 120.01 billion can be reproduced by carrying unrounded upstream values, but the answer does not state that convention. The difference leaves the forecast in rung 2.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:33:- Reader 2 on member v8: supported = yes. Ground: Reported first half: e109 (Q1 Data Center $75.25bn) + e5079 (Q2 Data Center $89.02bn) = F4 $164.27bn (matches e5309's $164bn). Q3 company revenue F9 = e5078 Q2 $96.22bn x (1 + 16%), the 16% a stated assumption benchmarked between guidance e2652 ($108bn, 12% sequential) and Pradhan's extrapolation e5056 ($113.6bn), with e3200 (Q2 beat), e2693, e5061 and constraint e5067; Q4 company revenue F11 = F9 x 1.15, a stated assumption resting on e3406 (hyperscale reacceleration in Q4), e5118, e5067, e5205. Data Center mix 93% (Q3) and 93.5% (Q4), stated assumptions stepping up from the reported Q2 mix of 92.5% (e3399; F3/F5 = 92.5%) with e5223 naming Data Center as the main growth driver. F14 $103.8bn + F15 $120.01bn = H2 $223.81bn; F17 = $388.08bn, inside rung 2 ($350bn to below $400bn). The band is robust to the assumptions: holding Q3 at guidance with the 92.5% mix (e3401, $99.9bn) and Q4 at analysts' 65% year-over-year growth on Q4 FY2026's $68bn (e5162, e2452) still gives about $368bn, also rung 2; Morningstar's $361bn (e2295) is in rung 2 too.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:34:- Reader 2 marks a defect: number misread (figures ['F6']): F6 records NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 guidance as 108 with qualifier \"none\" and no low/high, while its passage e2652 writes \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" (a $105.84bn-$110.16bn range, per e3346). The quote_number keeps the words, but the figure's qualifier differs from the passage. Minor: F6 is only a benchmark in F8's rests_on and is not on the arithmetic path to the outcome, so the forecast and its rung are unaffected.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:36:# The system text the writer was given\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:48:# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:76:# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:104:- e2652 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, with no Data Center compute revenue from China assumed.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:105:    Quote: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:191:- e4899 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:13952dff579190ce77353acd 2026-09-14] NVIDIA guided to approximately $108 billion in total revenue, plus or minus 2%, for FY2027 Q3, implying 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:192:    Quote: Looking ahead, NVIDIA expects revenues to reach approximately $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, representing 12% sequential growth from the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:238:- e4963 [party_statement; Stephen Guilfoyle; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:2fc71ee9730f9420d6d130bd 2026-09-14] NVIDIA projected current-quarter revenue of $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, versus consensus of $104.6 billion; the projection implied roughly 90% year-over-year growth.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:280:- e5005 [party_statement; Zacks analyst; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:955c64e5a5907651550e94b8 2026-09-15] NVIDIA guided fiscal Q3 2027 total revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, 12% above the prior quarter from the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:281:    Quote: The company expects revenues of around $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially from the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:325:- e5052 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA 2026-09-15; from gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b 2026-09-15] NVIDIA guided for Q3 FY2027 revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2% ($105.8 billion to $110.2 billion).\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:326:    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) expects third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:376:- e5081 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management guided Q3 FY2027 total revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:377:    Quote: Management then guided Q3 to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:424:- e5111 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA management 2026-08-26; from gnews:bc6926bf31d3a4d8006dce46 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management forecast fiscal Q3 2027 revenue of about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, up 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:425:    Quote: Management projects revenues of around $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, for the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially at the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:534:- e5223 [party_statement; The Motley Fool author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:12a943c49b0c41542e6110b5 2026-09-18] NVIDIA guided next-quarter total revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, citing continued strength in its AI Data Center business as the primary growth driver.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:535:    Quote: First, it guided total revenue for the next quarter to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and said growth would be driven primarily by continued strength in the AI data center business.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:639:- e5300 [party_statement; benzinga.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cbf072e65acf3391f77465fd 2026-09-18] NVIDIA forecast third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:640:    Quote: the company forecast third-quarter revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:652:- e5314 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:57a69374b8fa083ce36ecfb1 2026-09-18] NVIDIA guided FY2027 Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, about 12% above FY2027 Q2 revenue at the guidance midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:653:    Quote: Looking ahead, NVIDIA expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues to reach $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027, up 12% sequentially from the midpoint.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:677:- e2720 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 total revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:678:    Quote: Total revenue is expected to be $108 billion, plus or minus 2%.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:850:# The answer\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md:897:      \"evidence_id\": \"e2652\", \"quote_number\": \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\", \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"agent_message","text":"The two first readers agreed on Q10, so the registered STOP stands even though both adjudicators cleared it. I’m checking the controls against the actual packets and tracing why the same spending forecast flipped between reader groups."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nimport json,pathlib,re,collections,math\nbase=pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data'\\\\'')\nfolders=sorted(base.glob('\\\\''judging-v13s-blind*-2026-11-22'\\\\''))\nroot=base/'\\\\''judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\\\\''\ndef read(p):\n t=p.read_text()\n return json.loads(t[t.find('\\\\''{'\\\\''):t.rfind('\\\\''}'\\\\'')+1])\ndef answer(p):\n return p.read_text().split('\\\\''\\\\n# The answer\\\\n\\\\n'\\\\'',1)[-1].strip()\ndatasets=[(folder,'\\\\''reader'\\\\'') for folder in folders]+[(root,'\\\\''reread'\\\\'')]\nfor folder,prefix in datasets:\n key=read(folder/f'\\\\''{prefix}-key.json'\\\\'')\n directory=folder/f'\\\\''{prefix}-packets'\\\\''\n adj=folder/('\\\\''reread-adjudication'\\\\'' if prefix=='\\\\''reread'\\\\'' else '\\\\''adjudication'\\\\'')\n print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',folder.name,prefix,'\\\\''keys, packets, readers, adj:'\\\\'',[len(it) for it in [key,list(directory.glob('\\\\''*.md'\\\\'')),list(directory.glob('\\\\''*.codex.json'\\\\'')),list(directory.glob('\\\\''*.claude.json'\\\\'')),list(adj.glob('\\\\''*.md'\\\\'')),list(adj.glob('\\\\''*.codex.json'\\\\'')),list(adj.glob('\\\\''*.claude.json'\\\\''))]])\n num=collections.Counter()\n for pid,k in sorted(key.items()):\n  p=directory/f'\\\\''{pid}.md'\\\\''\n  body=read(folder/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/f\\\"{k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']}.json\\\") if prefix\"'!='\"'\\\\''reread'\\\\'' else json.loads(answer(p))\n  if prefix=='\\\\''reread'\\\\'':\n   dump=p.read_text()\n   pre,rest=dump.split('\\\\''# The request as the writer saw it\\\\n\\\\n'\\\\'',1)\n   user,raw=rest.split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n# The answer\\\\n\\\\n'\\\\'',1)\n   assert user==(folder/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''user.txt'\\\\'').read_text()\n   assert '\\\\''# The system text'\\\\'' not in pre and '\\\\''# The format rules'\\\\'' not in pre\n   if not k['\\\\''writer'\\\\''].startswith('\\\\''C-'\\\\''): assert body==read(folder/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/f\\\"{k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']}.json\\\")\n  matching=set()\n  for f in body['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n   if f['\\\\''kind'\\\\'']\"'!='\"'\\\\''source'\\\\'' or not isinstance(f.get('\\\\''quote_number'\\\\''),str) or '\\\\''plus or minus'\\\\'' not in f['\\\\''quote_number'\\\\''].lower() or f.get('\\\\''value'\\\\'') is None: continue\n   matching.add(f['\\\\''id'\\\\''])\n  num['\\\\''source figures'\\\\'']+=len(matching)\n  num['\\\\''replies'\\\\'']+=bool(matching)\n  for name in ['\\\\''codex'\\\\'','\\\\''claude'\\\\'']:\n   rpt=directory/f'\\\\''{pid}.{name}.json'\\\\''\n   if not rpt.exists():\n    print('\\\\''no reading'\\\\'',rpt)\n    continue\n   r=read(rpt)\n   defects=r['\\\\''defects'\\\\'']\n   mid=[x for x in defects if x['\\\\''type'\\\\'']=='\\\\''number misread'\\\\'' and set(x['\\\\''figures'\\\\''])&matching]\n   num['\\\\''marks'\\\\'']+=len(mid)\n   for x in mid:\n    print('\\\\'' mid'\\\\'',pid,k,name,x['\\\\''figures'\\\\''], x.get('\\\\''detail'\\\\'','\\\\'''\\\\''))\n    for person in ['\\\\''codex'\\\\'','\\\\''claude'\\\\'']:\n     a=adj/f'\\\\''{pid}.{person}.json'\\\\''\n     if a.exists():\n      rr=read(a)\n      print('\\\\''   adj'\\\\'',person,[(d['\\\\''decision'\\\\''],d['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']) for d in rr['\\\\''defects'\\\\''] if d['\\\\''type'\\\\'']=='\\\\''number misread'\\\\'' and set(d['\\\\''figures'\\\\''])&set(x['\\\\''figures'\\\\''])])\n     else: print('\\\\''   adj'\\\\'',person,'\\\\''absent'\\\\'')\n   if prefix=='\\\\''reread'\\\\'':\n    print('\\\\'' reread'\\\\'',pid,k['\\\\''case'\\\\''],k['\\\\''writer'\\\\''],name,[(m,x['\\\\''supported'\\\\'']) for m,x in r['\\\\''members'\\\\''].items()],[(x['\\\\''type'\\\\''],x['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']) for x in defects])\n print('\\\\''midpoint counts'\\\\'',dict(num))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nimport json,pathlib,re,collections,math\nbase=pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data'\\\\'')\nfolders=sorted(base.glob('\\\\''judging-v13s-blind*-2026-11-22'\\\\''))\nroot=base/'\\\\''judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\\\\''\ndef read(p):\n t=p.read_text()\n return json.loads(t[t.find('\\\\''{'\\\\''):t.rfind('\\\\''}'\\\\'')+1])\ndef answer(p):\n return p.read_text().split('\\\\''\\\\n# The answer\\\\n\\\\n'\\\\'',1)[-1].strip()\ndatasets=[(folder,'\\\\''reader'\\\\'') for folder in folders]+[(root,'\\\\''reread'\\\\'')]\nfor folder,prefix in datasets:\n key=read(folder/f'\\\\''{prefix}-key.json'\\\\'')\n directory=folder/f'\\\\''{prefix}-packets'\\\\''\n adj=folder/('\\\\''reread-adjudication'\\\\'' if prefix=='\\\\''reread'\\\\'' else '\\\\''adjudication'\\\\'')\n print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',folder.name,prefix,'\\\\''keys, packets, readers, adj:'\\\\'',[len(it) for it in [key,list(directory.glob('\\\\''*.md'\\\\'')),list(directory.glob('\\\\''*.codex.json'\\\\'')),list(directory.glob('\\\\''*.claude.json'\\\\'')),list(adj.glob('\\\\''*.md'\\\\'')),list(adj.glob('\\\\''*.codex.json'\\\\'')),list(adj.glob('\\\\''*.claude.json'\\\\''))]])\n num=collections.Counter()\n for pid,k in sorted(key.items()):\n  p=directory/f'\\\\''{pid}.md'\\\\''\n  body=read(folder/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/f\\\"{k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']}.json\\\") if prefix\"'!='\"'\\\\''reread'\\\\'' else json.loads(answer(p))\n  if prefix=='\\\\''reread'\\\\'':\n   dump=p.read_text()\n   pre,rest=dump.split('\\\\''# The request as the writer saw it\\\\n\\\\n'\\\\'',1)\n   user,raw=rest.split('\\\\''\\\\n\\\\n# The answer\\\\n\\\\n'\\\\'',1)\n   assert user==(folder/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''user.txt'\\\\'').read_text()\n   assert '\\\\''# The system text'\\\\'' not in pre and '\\\\''# The format rules'\\\\'' not in pre\n   if not k['\\\\''writer'\\\\''].startswith('\\\\''C-'\\\\''): assert body==read(folder/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''answers'\\\\''/f\\\"{k['\\\\''writer'\\\\'']}.json\\\")\n  matching=set()\n  for f in body['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']:\n   if f['\\\\''kind'\\\\'']\"'!='\"'\\\\''source'\\\\'' or not isinstance(f.get('\\\\''quote_number'\\\\''),str) or '\\\\''plus or minus'\\\\'' not in f['\\\\''quote_number'\\\\''].lower() or f.get('\\\\''value'\\\\'') is None: continue\n   matching.add(f['\\\\''id'\\\\''])\n  num['\\\\''source figures'\\\\'']+=len(matching)\n  num['\\\\''replies'\\\\'']+=bool(matching)\n  for name in ['\\\\''codex'\\\\'','\\\\''claude'\\\\'']:\n   rpt=directory/f'\\\\''{pid}.{name}.json'\\\\''\n   if not rpt.exists():\n    print('\\\\''no reading'\\\\'',rpt)\n    continue\n   r=read(rpt)\n   defects=r['\\\\''defects'\\\\'']\n   mid=[x for x in defects if x['\\\\''type'\\\\'']=='\\\\''number misread'\\\\'' and set(x['\\\\''figures'\\\\''])&matching]\n   num['\\\\''marks'\\\\'']+=len(mid)\n   for x in mid:\n    print('\\\\'' mid'\\\\'',pid,k,name,x['\\\\''figures'\\\\''], x.get('\\\\''detail'\\\\'','\\\\'''\\\\''))\n    for person in ['\\\\''codex'\\\\'','\\\\''claude'\\\\'']:\n     a=adj/f'\\\\''{pid}.{person}.json'\\\\''\n     if a.exists():\n      rr=read(a)\n      print('\\\\''   adj'\\\\'',person,[(d['\\\\''decision'\\\\''],d['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']) for d in rr['\\\\''defects'\\\\''] if d['\\\\''type'\\\\'']=='\\\\''number misread'\\\\'' and set(d['\\\\''figures'\\\\''])&set(x['\\\\''figures'\\\\''])])\n     else: print('\\\\''   adj'\\\\'',person,'\\\\''absent'\\\\'')\n   if prefix=='\\\\''reread'\\\\'':\n    print('\\\\'' reread'\\\\'',pid,k['\\\\''case'\\\\''],k['\\\\''writer'\\\\''],name,[(m,x['\\\\''supported'\\\\'']) for m,x in r['\\\\''members'\\\\''].items()],[(x['\\\\''type'\\\\''],x['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']) for x in defects])\n print('\\\\''midpoint counts'\\\\'',dict(num))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"\n judging-v13s-blind-2026-11-22 reader keys, packets, readers, adj: [12, 12, 12, 12, 9, 9, 9]\n mid R05 {'case': 'P-v8', 'writer': 'claude'} codex ['F5'] e2652 gives $108 billion plus or minus 2%, a range of $105.84 billion to $110.16 billion. F5 records the midpoint with qualifier 'none' and leaves both bounds empty, although the quote and reasoning preserve the range.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F5'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F5'])]\n mid R06 {'case': 'P-v4', 'writer': 'codex'} codex ['F4'] e2652 gives $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%. F4 preserves the range in the quoted text but records the midpoint with no qualifier or bounds.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F4'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F4'])]\n mid R10 {'case': 'P', 'writer': 'codex'} codex ['F5'] e2652 gives $108 billion plus or minus 2%. F5 retains that qualifier in its quote but stores an unqualified point with no bounds. The forecast explanation correctly uses $108 billion as the midpoint.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F5'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F5'])]\n mid R12 {'case': 'P-v8', 'writer': 'codex'} codex ['F8'] e2652 gives $108.0 billion as the guidance midpoint with a plus-or-minus 2% range. F8 quotes that range but records no bounds and sets the qualifier to none.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F8'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F8'])]\nmidpoint counts {'source figures': 5, 'replies': 5, 'marks': 4}\n\n judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22 reader keys, packets, readers, adj: [12, 12, 12, 12, 6, 6, 6]\n mid R06 {'case': 'P-v8', 'writer': 'codex'} codex ['F7'] e2652 gives Q3 company revenue guidance of $108 billion plus or minus 2%. F7 preserves the tolerance in the quote, but its numeric fields give an unqualified $108 billion point without identifying it as the midpoint.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F7'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F7'])]\n mid R09 {'case': 'P', 'writer': 'claude'} codex ['F4'] e2720 gives $108bn plus or minus 2%. F4 retains that qualifier in the quote but records an unqualified $108bn point with no bounds. The implied guidance range is $105.84bn to $110.16bn.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F4'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F4'])]\n mid R10 {'case': 'P-v4', 'writer': 'codex'} codex ['F3'] F3 records the $108 billion guidance with qualifier 'none' and leaves the bounds empty. e2652 gives $108 billion plus or minus 2%, a qualifier preserved in the quote but omitted from the structured figure. The calculation correctly uses $108 billion as the midpoint.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F3'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F3'])]\nmidpoint counts {'source figures': 5, 'replies': 5, 'marks': 3}\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 reader keys, packets, readers, adj: [12, 12, 12, 12, 6, 6, 6]\n mid R02 {'case': 'P', 'writer': 'codex'} codex ['F4'] F4 quotes e2652's $108 billion plus or minus 2% guidance but records the qualifier as none and leaves both bounds empty. The forecast reasoning correctly uses $108 billion as the midpoint, so the omitted qualifier does not change either forecast.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F4'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F4'])]\n mid R09 {'case': 'P-v4', 'writer': 'claude'} codex ['F5'] e2652 gives $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%. F5 preserves that range in its quotation but records no qualifier or bounds in the numeric fields. The forecast correctly uses $108.0 billion as the midpoint.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F5'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F5'])]\nmidpoint counts {'source figures': 5, 'replies': 5, 'marks': 2}\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 reread keys, packets, readers, adj: [14, 14, 14, 14, 4, 4, 4]\n reread Q01 P codex codex [('v4', 'yes'), ('v8', 'yes')] []\n reread Q01 P codex claude [('v4', 'yes'), ('v8', 'yes')] []\n reread Q02 E codex codex [('v41', 'yes')] []\n reread Q02 E codex claude [('v41', 'yes')] []\n reread Q03 O codex codex [('v29', 'yes')] []\n reread Q03 O codex claude [('v29', 'yes')] []\n reread Q04 P-v4 codex codex [('v4', 'yes')] []\n reread Q04 P-v4 codex claude [('v4', 'yes')] []\n reread Q05 P-v8 claude codex [('v8', 'yes')] []\n reread Q05 P-v8 claude claude [('v8', 'yes')] []\n reread Q06 E C-disjoint codex [('v41', 'no')] [('broken structure', ['F8']), ('wrong arithmetic', ['F1', 'F2', 'F3'])]\n reread Q06 E C-disjoint claude [('v41', 'no')] [('broken structure', ['F8'])]\n reread Q07 S claude codex [('v6', 'no')] [('wrong arithmetic', ['F1', 'F2', 'F3', 'F6', 'F8', 'F9', 'F10'])]\n reread Q07 S claude claude [('v6', 'yes')] [('two forecasts of one quarter', ['F7', 'F6', 'F8', 'F9', 'F10'])]\n reread Q08 S codex codex [('v6', 'yes')] []\n reread Q08 S codex claude [('v6', 'yes')] []\n reread Q09 O claude codex [('v29', 'no')] [('number misread', ['F2']), ('number misread', ['F10'])]\n reread Q09 O claude claude [('v29', 'yes')] []\n mid Q10 {'case': 'P-v8', 'writer': 'codex'} codex ['F6'] e2652 gives $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%. F6 preserves that wording in the quote but records the guidance as an unqualified point with no bounds.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F6']), ('reject', ['F6'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F6']), ('reject', ['F6'])]\n reread Q10 P-v8 codex codex [('v8', 'yes')] [('number misread', ['F6']), ('wrong arithmetic', ['F15', 'F11', 'F13'])]\n mid Q10 {'case': 'P-v8', 'writer': 'codex'} claude ['F6'] F6 records NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 guidance as 108 with qualifier \"none\" and no low/high, while its passage e2652 writes \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" (a $105.84bn-$110.16bn range, per e3346). The quote_number keeps the words, but the figure's qualifier differs from the passage. Minor: F6 is only a benchmark in F8's rests_on and is not on the arithmetic path to the outcome, so the forecast and its rung are unaffected.\n   adj codex [('reject', ['F6']), ('reject', ['F6'])]\n   adj claude [('reject', ['F6']), ('reject', ['F6'])]\n reread Q10 P-v8 codex claude [('v8', 'yes')] [('number misread', ['F6'])]\n reread Q11 P-v4 claude codex [('v4', 'yes')] []\n reread Q11 P-v4 claude claude [('v4', 'yes')] []\n reread Q12 E C-overlap codex [('v41', 'yes')] [('wrong arithmetic', ['F1', 'F2', 'F3']), ('two forecasts of one quarter', ['F7', 'F8'])]\n reread Q12 E C-overlap claude [('v41', 'yes')] [('two forecasts of one quarter', ['F7', 'F8'])]\n reread Q13 P claude codex [('v4', 'yes'), ('v8', 'yes')] []\n reread Q13 P claude claude [('v4', 'yes'), ('v8', 'yes')] []\n reread Q14 E claude codex [('v41', 'yes')] [('wrong arithmetic', ['F1', 'F2', 'F3'])]\n reread Q14 E claude claude [('v41', 'yes')] []\nmidpoint counts {'source figures': 5, 'replies': 5, 'marks': 2}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
