{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^[#]|95–100|roughly .*190|Zacks|e2759|e5376|e4082|e1416|\"F(6|7|8|9|10|13|14)\"'\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.md | cut -c 1-360\nrg -n '\"'^[#]|F(15|16|17|18)|90%|e4508|e1549|e5014|e5376|e1416'\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.md | cut -c 1-340\nrg -Hn 'supported|why|ground' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/{reread-packets/Q08,adjudication/R07}.{claude,codex}.json\"","aggregated_output":"1:# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, SMALL-DESIGN.md §5)\n33:# The request as the writer saw it\n51:- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n73:- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n81:- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n117:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n137:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n144:- e5150 [fact; Zacks Equity Research; original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] The expanded AWS partnership calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027–2028.\n162:- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n170:# The answer\n270:      \"id\": \"F6\",\n283:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Microsoft states no calendar-2026 total in the evidence. Its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% year over year, and its $678bn Azure backlog supports a continuing ramp: carrying the growth pace through the year at roughly 15% sequential increases a quarter gives about $155bn on the reported quarter's basis (\n284:      \"rests_on\": [\"F4\", \"F5\", \"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\", \"e4405\"],\n289:      \"id\": \"F7\",\n300:      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\", \"F6\"],\n308:      \"id\": \"F8\",\n327:      \"id\": \"F9\",\n346:      \"id\": \"F10\",\n373:      \"evidence_id\": \"e1416\",\n392:      \"evidence_id\": \"e5376\",\n403:      \"id\": \"F13\",\n416:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A \n417:      \"rests_on\": [\"F11\", \"F12\", \"e4082\", \"e5014\", \"e1649\"],\n422:      \"id\": \"F14\",\n433:      \"inputs\": [\"F7\", \"F13\"],\n447:      \"reasons\": \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $\n449:      \"cites\": [\"e5014\", \"e1254\", \"e1549\", \"e1649\", \"e922\", \"e4405\", \"e1416\", \"e4082\", \"e2759\", \"e5376\", \"e4567\", \"e4566\", \"e1856\", \"e2458\", \"e122\", \"e1545\", \"e5292\", \"e2081\", \"e4807\"],\n459:        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1416\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n460:        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4082\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n461:        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2759\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n467:        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5376\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n1:# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, SMALL-DESIGN.md §5)\n33:# The request as the writer saw it\n51:- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n55:- e1549 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, as reported by Joel South; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n117:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n137:    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n162:- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n170:# The answer\n183:      \"evidence_id\": \"e5014\",\n259:      \"evidence_id\": \"e1549\",\n354:      \"evidence_id\": \"e5376\",\n397:      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that data centers account for 90% of combined capital expenditure. Zacks identifies AI infrastructure as a large use of the group's spending, and the Meta AI capex estimate is close to its total capex guidance. I use that comparison as evidence of a high share and allow for other capital spending across\n428:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $670.5 billion in CY2026 data-center capex, which falls in rung 3. The closest market benchmark by company coverage is Zacks's $720 billion to $745 billion estimate for all capex, numerically in rung 4. The company spending revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support $745 billion of total ca\n430:      \"cites\": [\"e5014\", \"e2458\", \"e1254\", \"e1649\", \"e1549\", \"e3686\", \"e922\", \"e4404\", \"e1947\", \"e5376\", \"e122\", \"e5292\", \"e5150\"],\n432:        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5014\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n436:        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1549\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n441:        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5376\", \"push\": \"none\"},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:5:      \"ground\": \"The answer uses $745 billion in total capex, the high end of e5014, and assumes 90% goes to data centers based on e5014 and the Meta comparison in e1549 and e3686. That yields $670.5 billion, within rung 3's $600 billion to under $700 billion band.\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json:6:      \"why_not\": \"\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"why\": \"The rung rests entirely on F12's 90% data-center share: share(745, 90) = 670.5, rung 3, but rung 3 holds only while the share is below 700 / 745 = 94.0%. The shown passages put the share near 100%, not 90%: e1947 says the four 'will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year', against the same four's 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion' of capital expenditures (e5014; e2079), which is at least 98%; NVIDIA's top-five figure is written both as total 'CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers ... nearly $800 billion in 2026' (e2759) and as 'nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026' (e5376), matching BofA's five-company total capex of 'roughly $795 billion' (e5055); e4082 puts 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year' and e1416 says tech giants 'will spend an estimated $750 billion this year'. The answer's own stated basis, Meta's '$130 billion of AI capex' (e3686) against its '$125 billion to $145 billion' guidance (e1549), gives 90% to 104%, about 96% at the midpoint, which is above the 94% cutoff. The cut to 90% to 'allow for other capital spending' has no passage behind it (e4404 says only that 'Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited'), so the forecast below $700 billion does not follow from the passages; with any share the passages support (94% or more), the same F11 of $745 billion gives rung 4. The arithmetic is correct and the share is written as its own figure as the format rule asks ('write the deduction or share as its own figure'), so no format defect arises; the failure is that the decisive assumption is not supported.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"no\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json:5:      \"why\": \"Zacks supports $745 billion in total spending, describing a “large portion” as directed toward AI infrastructure. Applying the stated 90% share gives $670.5 billion, which is in rung 3. But rung 3 requires a share below about 94%, and the cited passages give no reason to expect the four companies’ share to fall below that cutoff. Meta’s $130 billion in AI spending is about 96% of the midpoint of its total spending guidance, and the AWS growth passage gives no spending breakdown. The 90% assumption is explicit, but the evidence does not support the deduction that puts the forecast in rung 3.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F13 = F11 x F12 = 745 x 90% = 670.5, inside rung 3 (600 to below 700). F11 (745 total capex) is a stated assumption: the top of Zacks's roughly 720-745 range (e5014), below the 760 plan (e2458). The company guidance in view backs it (Alphabet 195-205 e1254, Amazon 220 e1649, Meta 125-145 e1549, which leaves about 175-205 for Microsoft), and the slippage reasons cite e122 and e5292. F12 (90% data-center share) is the writer's own stated assumption, and the answer says the packet has no group-wide split. Its only grounds are Meta's 130 AI capex (e3686) against its 125-145 guidance (e1549), a ratio of about 90-104%, and Zacks's 'large portion' wording (e5014). The rung depends on this assumption: at a share of about 94% or more, the same 745 total lands in rung 4. The answer names the spending mix as its biggest uncertainty near that cutoff.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.md | sed -n '1,78p'\nrg -n 'midpoint|round(ed|ing)|qualifier|relative' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | head -42\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/system.txt | sed -n '1,26p'\nrg -n 'Reviewer|blind|adjudic' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n     2\t\n     3\tTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\n     4\tbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n     5\t\n     6\tFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no): the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions.\n     7\tFor each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the passage words or the format rule that decides it. The\n     8\tdefect types are:\n     9\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    10\t  format rules permit.\n    11\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs.\n    12\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    13\t  includes it.\n    14\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    15\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    16\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value\n    17\t  of the quarter.\n    18\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    19\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    20\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    21\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure depending on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome not of the\n    22\t  question's quantity, a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps, with\n    23\t  the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    24\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of the question's quantity (a ruling).\n    25\t\n    26\tReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n    27\t \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n    28\t\n    29\t# The two readings\n    30\t- Reader 1 on member v8: supported = yes. Ground: e109 and e5079 establish $164.27 billion in first-half Data Center revenue. The stated assumptions apply 16% and 15% sequential company revenue growth to the $96.22 billion Q2 base in e5078, then use Data Center shares of 93% and 93.5%. The assumptions draw on guidance and the prior beat (e2652, e3200, e5056), the Rubin ramp and supply constraints (e2693, e5061, e3406, e5118, e5067, e5205), and the rising Data Center share (e3399, e5223). They produce about $388 billion, within rung 2's $350 billion to under $400 billion band. Morningstar's separate $361 billion estimate is directly sourced to e2295.\n    31\t- Reader 1 marks a defect: number misread (figures ['F6']): e2652 gives $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%. F6 preserves that wording in the quote but records the guidance as an unqualified point with no bounds.\n    32\t- Reader 1 marks a defect: wrong arithmetic (figures ['F15', 'F11', 'F13']): The listed inputs give 128.36 × 93.5% = 120.0166, which rounds to 120.02 billion. The stated 120.01 billion can be reproduced by carrying unrounded upstream values, but the answer does not state that convention. The difference leaves the forecast in rung 2.\n    33\t- Reader 2 on member v8: supported = yes. Ground: Reported first half: e109 (Q1 Data Center $75.25bn) + e5079 (Q2 Data Center $89.02bn) = F4 $164.27bn (matches e5309's $164bn). Q3 company revenue F9 = e5078 Q2 $96.22bn x (1 + 16%), the 16% a stated assumption benchmarked between guidance e2652 ($108bn, 12% sequential) and Pradhan's extrapolation e5056 ($113.6bn), with e3200 (Q2 beat), e2693, e5061 and constraint e5067; Q4 company revenue F11 = F9 x 1.15, a stated assumption resting on e3406 (hyperscale reacceleration in Q4), e5118, e5067, e5205. Data Center mix 93% (Q3) and 93.5% (Q4), stated assumptions stepping up from the reported Q2 mix of 92.5% (e3399; F3/F5 = 92.5%) with e5223 naming Data Center as the main growth driver. F14 $103.8bn + F15 $120.01bn = H2 $223.81bn; F17 = $388.08bn, inside rung 2 ($350bn to below $400bn). The band is robust to the assumptions: holding Q3 at guidance with the 92.5% mix (e3401, $99.9bn) and Q4 at analysts' 65% year-over-year growth on Q4 FY2026's $68bn (e5162, e2452) still gives about $368bn, also rung 2; Morningstar's $361bn (e2295) is in rung 2 too.\n    34\t- Reader 2 marks a defect: number misread (figures ['F6']): F6 records NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 guidance as 108 with qualifier \"none\" and no low/high, while its passage e2652 writes \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" (a $105.84bn-$110.16bn range, per e3346). The quote_number keeps the words, but the figure's qualifier differs from the passage. Minor: F6 is only a benchmark in F8's rests_on and is not on the arithmetic path to the outcome, so the forecast and its rung are unaffected.\n    35\t\n    36\t# The system text the writer was given\n    37\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n    38\t\n    39\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n    40\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n    41\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n    42\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n    43\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n    44\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n    45\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\t# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)\n    49\t{\n    50\t \"figures\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\",\n    51\t \"figures/id\": \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\",\n    52\t \"figures/what\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\",\n    53\t \"figures/what/measure\": \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\",\n    54\t \"figures/what/period\": \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\",\n    55\t \"figures/kind\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\",\n    56\t \"figures/basis\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\",\n    57\t \"figures/value\": \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\",\n    58\t \"figures/low\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    59\t \"figures/high\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    60\t \"figures/unit\": \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\",\n    61\t \"figures/evidence_id\": \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\",\n    62\t \"figures/quote_number\": \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\",\n    63\t \"figures/qualifier\": \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\",\n    64\t \"figures/inputs\": \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\",\n    65\t \"figures/operation\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\",\n    66\t \"figures/basis_text\": \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\",\n    67\t \"figures/rests_on\": \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\",\n    68\t \"figures/outcome_for\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\",\n    69\t \"figures/reference_for\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\",\n    70\t \"answers/shape\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\",\n    71\t \"answers/rung\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\",\n    72\t \"answers/reference_unavailable\": \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\",\n    73\t \"answers/why\": \"Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.\"\n    74\t}\n    75\t\n    76\t# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)\n    77\t[Family] members:\n    78\tThesis v8 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Data Center segment revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n33:    ('relative', _CUR + _NUM + _SC + r'\\s*,?\\s*\\(?\\s*(?:plus or minus|\\+/-|±)\\s*' + _NUM + r'\\s*%\\s*\\)?'),\n63:        if form == 'relative':\n152:FIGURE_FIELDS = ('id', 'what', 'kind', 'basis', 'value', 'low', 'high', 'unit', 'quote_number', 'qualifier', 'inputs', 'operation',\n397:        if kind != 'source' and (f.get('evidence') or f.get('quote_number') or (f.get('qualifier') or 'none') != 'none'):\n398:            errors.append(('S7', f'{fid}: evidence_id, quote_number and qualifier are for source figures only'))\n487:        q = f.get('qualifier') or 'none'\n496:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: the quote writes \"{written[0] if written else \"none\"}\" with \"{qn}\", but the qualifier is {q}'))\n750:        elif (f.get('qualifier') or 'none') != 'none':\n751:            recorded.append({'type': 'qualified source', 'figure': fid, 'qualifier': f['qualifier']})\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n1:# judging-v13s blind test, third run after D742: result (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D742])\n6:- **Both adjudicators reject that mark.** The schema the writer was given says the rung is \"for a range outcome, a band it overlaps,\n15:- **Readers and adjudicators:** six packets drew a disagreement. Every disputed mark was settled, and every one was rejected by both\n16:  adjudicators. No defect was upheld, and no split remains.\n21:**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`, $0 at the provider. All Codex writers read only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n35:## 2. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n37:**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n40:- two \"plus or minus 2%\" midpoint marks (R02, R09) and a rounding difference within tolerance (R08). In all, both adjudicators\n55:- Every other forecast is supported by both readers or both adjudicators.\n63:  given. Both adjudicators corrected it from the format rule.\n73:  - wrong numbers in prose, which this run's adjudications did not raise;\n76:## 4. The blinded re-read (D743, Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\"; `reread-packets/`, `reread-adjudication/`, `REREAD-TALLY.json`)\n86:  - Both adjudicators rejected it. The schema the writer was given says \"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high\n88:  - The same mark has been raised about 12 times across the four readings, by single readers until now. Every adjudication rejected it.\n90:  adjudication packet carries them and its definitions say \"allowing what the format rules permit\". Both stops caused by the instrument\n96:  - S's Codex reply (90% share) was settled \"not supported\" by both adjudicators in the first reading, and is \"supported\" by both fresh\n100:- **No settled defect.** Every adjudicated mark on Q09, Q10 and Q14 was rejected by both adjudicators. Q07's two marks split.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q*.{claude,codex}.json; do rg -Hn 'supported|decision|why|type' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"for f in poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q*.{claude,codex}.json; do rg -Hn 'supported|decision|why|type' \\\"\"'$f\"; done'","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The forecast follows from the passages and the stated assumptions. F7 = sum(F1 $195-205bn from e1254 'a range of $195 billion to $205 billion', F2 $125-145bn from e1549 'FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion', F3 $220bn from e1649 'a new projection of $220 billion', F6 $175bn) = $715-745bn, which is correct. F14 = share(F7, F13 95-100%) = 715 x 0.95 = 679.25 up to 745 x 1.00 = 745, also correct. The midpoint is about $712bn. The range overlaps rungs 3 and 4, and the reasons explain the choice of rung 4, which the format allows: 'for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons'. F6 is a stated estimate with grounds in the passages. A run-rate from e922's '$30.88 billion, up 84%' at about 15% sequential growth gives about $154bn. The outside tallies less the other three companies' guidance give the residual the system text allows: 'a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence'. That residual is $177.5-205bn at the centres, so $175bn is at or below it. F13 follows the format rule 'write the deduction or share as its own figure'. It is a labelled estimate, and the passages back it: the same NVIDIA top-five figure appears as total capex ('CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion', e2759/e5081) and as data-center capex ('will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures', e5376/e4766). e4082 puts 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion', next to the four companies' $720-760bn capex tallies. Caveats: rung 4 needs a share of roughly 96% or more at F7's midpoint, so the share decides the rung. e5014's 'a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure' is weaker than the reasons suggest. The reference F8 is total capex, not data-center spending.\"}},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.claude.json:3:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\", \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The defect is defined as 'a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs'. Of the figures listed, F1-F3 and F8-F10 are sources and F6 is an estimate ('estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text'). None is computed. The computed figures on the path check out: F7 = 195+125+220+175 to 205+145+220+175 = 715-745, and F14 = 715 x 95/100 = 679.25 to 745 x 100/100 = 745. The reader is right that F6's basis_text 'imply roughly $175-190bn at their centres' leaves out F10's centre (760 - 555 = 205). That is a loose sentence inside an assumption, not a wrong computed figure. It also leans conservative: the correct residual of $177.5-205bn would only support a Microsoft estimate of $175bn or more.\"},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.claude.json:4:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 2\", \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\", \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"F7 is the only forecast figure for the four companies' combined CY2026 capex, and it alone supplies that quantity in the calculation (F14 = share(F7, F13)). F8, F9 and F10 are source figures with basis third_party, and they sit beneath it through F6's rests_on. The format rule allows this: 'Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period ... (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine)'. Inside F6's assumption the tallies serve only as a benchmark for one company's estimate, through the identity the system text allows ('a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it'). The writer then takes $175bn, 'between the two' legs, rather than any tally as the total. This matches the ruling that a figure 'used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the calculation'. The dollar values in the assumption are outside parties' figures, not a second forecast of the total by the writer. Nothing depends on itself: F7 rests on F6, F6 rests on F8-F10, and no figure rests on F7. Reader 2's point that rung 4 depends on the tally leg is a sensitivity of the estimate, not this defect.\"}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v29\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The calculation follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions. e2620 gives 'equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026' (F1). e2889 is the company's statement on the same day (2026-08-26) that 'it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year'. The answer reads this as the part of the commitment book due in the rest of FY2027. That reading is an inference, but it is the natural one: the figure is tied to a time ('for the rest of its fiscal year'), was given alongside the reported balance, and is smaller than it. The answer states the reading openly ('the company's own schedule for the current half') and names what would reverse it ('a large part of the $18 billion slipping into next year'). F4 ($2-13 billion) is marked as the writer's own estimate, with its assumption written out. It counts the staged money in e1970 (SB Energy's second $1.5 billion), e1919 (Lancium's additional $1 billion) and e4648 (an Anthropic anchor of 'as much as $10 billion'). It leaves out deals paid at closing (e2207, e3474, e1957), and e2643 and e4487 support that choice. The arithmetic checks: 25 - 18 = 7; 7 + 2 = 9; 7 + 13 = 20. The range $9-20 billion lies wholly below the $25 billion boundary, so rung 1 holds the outcome. Reader 1 objects that the forecast 'has not established' a year-end balance below $25 billion. The system text rules that ground out: a rung is the most likely outcome, not a proved one, and '\\\"not established\\\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay'. The answer also explains why Bank of America's $70 billion is a different measure: e4729's '>$42 billion' already bought plus the $25 billion still owed comes close to it.\"}},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.claude.json:3:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F2\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"A number misread needs the number, scale, sign or qualifier to differ from the quote. e2889's quote writes '$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year' with no qualifier before the number. F2 records value 18, unit USD bn, quote_number '$18 billion', qualifier 'none', so all four match. Reader 1's complaint is about how the answer uses the figure, as a payment schedule that reduces the July 26 balance. That is a question of interpretation, judged under whether the member is supported. It is not a misread of the number.\"},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.claude.json:4:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F10\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The format rule says the qualifier is the one 'the quote writes just before the number'. e1919's quote writes 'followed by an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting particular go[als]'. In the quote, 'up to' appears only before the $3 billion total ('will spend up to $3 billion in Lancium'). 'Up to $1 billion' is the passage's summary wording, not the quote's. So F10's quote_number '$1 billion' with qualifier 'none' matches the quote. Putting the full $1 billion in the low end of the estimate is a judgement inside F4, not a misread of a source figure.\"}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"Every link resolves and recomputes within the format's tolerance. F4 = F2 $75.25bn (e109 'the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion') + F3 $89.02bn (e5079 'Data Center alone at $89.02 billion') = 164.27. F9 = apply_growth(F5 $96.22bn, e5078 'Q2 FY2027 revenue landed at $96.22 billion', 16%) = 111.6152, stated 111.62. F11 = 111.62 x 1.15 = 128.363, stated 128.36. F14 = share(111.62, 93) = 103.807, stated 103.8. F15 = share(128.36, 93.5) = 120.0166, stated 120.01, inside 'within 0.5% (at least 0.1)'. F16 = 103.8 + 120.01 = 223.81 (Q3 + Q4 = H2); F17 = 164.27 + 223.81 = 388.08, inside rung 2 ('at least $350 billion and less than $400 billion'). The assumptions are stated estimates with sources. The 16% Q3 pace puts Q3 above the guidance range ('$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%', e2652), and the passages support that beat: e3200 ('actual revenue exceeded that midpoint by $5.2 billion') and e5056 ('revenue could reach roughly $113.6 billion, compared with the current high end of about $110.2 billion'), with supply limits (e5067) given as the reason for a smaller beat than Pradhan's. The 15% Q4 pace rests on e3406 ('growth in its hyperscale business ... should reaccelerate in the fiscal fourth quarter as Vera Rubin supply builds'), and the rising mix on e3399 ('The share rose to 89.8% ... then 92.5%') and on e5223 (growth 'driven primarily by continued strength in the AI data center business'). F9 is the one company Q3 revenue figure on the calculation path. F6 and F7 appear only in F8's rests_on as benchmarks, which the ruling allows. The reference F1 ($361bn, e2295 'we estimate it will be $361 billion in fiscal 2027') is a quoted outside source that also falls in rung 2.\"}},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.claude.json:3:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F6\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"The quote reads 'Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%.' The format rule for quote_number says ''$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value.' F6 copies '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%' in full and gives its midpoint, 108, as the value. That is an explicitly permitted form, not a misread. Under the format, 'plus or minus 2%' belongs in quote_number, not in the qualifier field, which is 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after)'. No such word appears, so qualifier 'none' is correct.\"},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.claude.json:4:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\", \"figures\": [\"F15\", \"F11\", \"F13\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"share(F11 128.36, F13 93.5) = 120.0166. The stated 120.01 differs by 0.0066. The format rule says 'The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %)'. A gap of 0.0066 is far inside a 0.1 tolerance, so the figure equals its operation as the format defines equality. No stated rounding convention is required.\"},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.claude.json:5:  {\"reader\": \"Reader 2\", \"type\": \"number misread\", \"figures\": [\"F6\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"e2652 writes '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%'. The quote_number rule permits this expression 'in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value'. F6 gives the midpoint, 108, so the absence of low and high follows the format. 'Plus or minus 2%' is not a qualifier as the format defines one ('the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after)'). The quote has no such word, so 'none' matches the passage. The defect type itself excludes 'what the format rules permit'.\"}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.claude.json:1:{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"F1 = $20 billion FY2027 base is quoted in e3660 ('still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027'), and e4856 ('more than double that number in fiscal year 2028', summarised as Vera CPU revenue against the $20 billion FY2027 figure) and e4691 ('Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year') tie that base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue. The stated assumption F7 (85% growth) discounts the management doubling (F2) for time and execution, as the system text requires for a multi-quarter target, and tempers it toward the outside market pace (e4687 '50%'; e4857 '$35 billion to $170 billion'), with adoption breadth (e4689, e5044, e4692) for and AMD's benchmark claims (e5399-e5402, e5404) against. F8 = apply_growth(20, 85) = 20 x 1.85 = 37, which lies in rung 3's band 'at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion'. The forecast follows from the passages and the stated assumption.\"}},\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.claude.json:2: \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\", \"figures\": [\"F1\", \"F2\", \"F3\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"Wrong arithmetic is 'a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs'. F3 = product(F1, F2) = 20 x 2 = 40, and F3 records 40, so it equals its operation within the rule's 0.5%. F2's value of 2 for the quoted 'double' is what the format prescribes ('x: a multiple (double = 2)'; quote_number 'double'), and the quote's 'more than' is recorded where the format puts it, in F2's qualifier ('the qualifier the quote writes just before the number'); the answer's reasons keep the bound ('That gives more than $40 billion (F3)'). That F3 is a lower bound rather than a point, or that the bound leaves $50 billion or more open, is not a mismatch between a computed figure and its inputs, and F3 is only the reference: the outcome F8 does not use it.\"}]}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"no\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:5:      \"why\": \"The $679.25–745 billion range follows arithmetically from F7 and F13 and overlaps rung 4. The passages do not support F13’s assumption that 95–100% of the four companies’ total capital expenditure goes to data centers. e5014 says only that “a large portion” goes to AI infrastructure, and e1649 explains Amazon’s spending increase. e1416 and e4082 do not identify the same four companies, while e5376 explicitly covers five. F8 is a reference for total capital expenditure and cannot establish the data-center share.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:13:      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:14:      \"why\": \"F6 says the outside tallies imply “roughly $175-190bn at their centres.” The other three companies’ guidance has a combined midpoint of $555 billion. Subtracting it from the cited tallies gives $177.5 billion, about $190 billion and $205 billion. The last result follows from e2458’s “$760 billion in capex in 2026 alone” and falls outside the claimed range. The error is in the subtraction described in F6’s reasoning. F6’s final $175 billion is a judgment.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:18:      \"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:20:      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q07.codex.json:21:      \"why\": \"The rule says to “Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period” and to look inside stated assumptions for a second dollar value. F6 uses the outside totals “less the other three companies’ guidance” to derive a Microsoft residual. That subtraction uses the tallies as dollar values for the same combined CY2026 capital expenditure that F7 forecasts by adding Microsoft back. The outside figures could serve as comparisons, but here they supply a dollar input to the forecast through F6.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"no\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:5:      \"why\": \"e2620 reports $25 billion in commitments as of July 26. e2889 says NVIDIA had $18 billion committed for the rest of its fiscal year. The forecast subtracts all $18 billion from the July balance, but the passage does not say that the full amount was included in that balance or would be paid by year-end. The answer presents both assumptions as NVIDIA's payment schedule. The $9–20 billion forecast and rung 1 depend on that unsupported deduction.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:13:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:14:      \"why\": \"e2889 says it has \\\"$18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year.\\\" F2 copies the amount, scale, sign and qualifier correctly. The defect is the unsupported interpretation of what the $18 billion represents.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:18:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:20:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q09.codex.json:21:      \"why\": \"The format requires the qualifier from the passage's quote. In e1919, \\\"up to\\\" modifies the $3 billion total, while the tranche is quoted as \\\"an additional $1 billion based on the company meeting\\\" conditions. F10 preserves the amount and identifies it as milestone-linked. Using the full tranche in the low estimate is a forecast assumption.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:5:      \"why\": \"The reported first half totals $164.27 billion in Data Center revenue (e109 and e5079). Applying the stated 16% and 15% sequential company revenue growth assumptions and the 93% and 93.5% Data Center shares yields about $388.08 billion for FY2027, within rung 2's $350 billion to below $400 billion band. The prior guidance beat and initial Rubin shipments support a Q3 above guidance (e3200 and e2693). Expected Q4 hyperscaler acceleration and supply constraints support further growth at a constrained pace (e3406 and e5067). The rising Data Center share and its role as the primary driver of Q3 growth support the assumed mix gains (e3399 and e5223). Morningstar's separate $361 billion estimate is directly quoted in e2295.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:13:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:14:      \"why\": \"F6 quotes e2652's full expression, '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%'. The format permits that expression to be 'given as low and high or its midpoint as value'. The $108 billion point is the midpoint.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:18:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:20:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:21:      \"why\": \"The listed inputs give 128.36 × 93.5 ÷ 100 = 120.0166. F15's reported 120.01 differs by $0.0066 billion and satisfies the rule that 'The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %)'.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:25:      \"type\": \"number misread\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:27:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json:28:      \"why\": \"F6 preserves '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%' in quote_number and uses the permitted midpoint as value. The qualifier field is for 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or \\\\'or more\\\\' / \\\\'or less\\\\' just after)'. The quote has no such qualifier, so 'none' is correct.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:4:      \"supported\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:5:      \"why\": \"Management's $20 billion FY2027 base and doubling outlook, broad planned Vera deployments, and CPU market growth support a strong FY2028 forecast. The answer explains why it discounts the outlook to 85% growth for execution risk and AMD competition. Applying that estimate to $20 billion gives $37 billion, within rung 3.\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:11:      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:13:      \"decision\": \"reject\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q14.codex.json:14:      \"why\": \"The format says the source value is 'the number as written,' stores 'more than' separately as the qualifier, and defines product as 'a × b × …'. F3 correctly computes 20 × 2 = 40 from the recorded values. The passage's 'more than double' makes $40 billion a threshold and leaves $50 billion or more possible, so the qualifier matters when interpreting the reference.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,25p' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '475,555p'\nnl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '328,351p'\nrg -n 'decision|errors|case|writer' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,25p' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '475,555p'\nnl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '328,351p'\nrg -n 'decision|errors|case|writer' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json\"","aggregated_output":"\"\"\"judging-v13s checks: the small format (SMALL-DESIGN.md §2 [52] and [54], D737, D738). Harness-only. Code refuses an answer only for\nthe five named defects (S1 a number misread, S2 wrong arithmetic, S3/S8 a part above its whole or added into it, S4 two forecasts of one\nquarter, S5 an own estimate passed off as the market's) and for broken structure (S6, S7), and only where the calculation's own\nstructure decides it (D738). Everything else is recorded for readers.\n\nfrom_answer(data, family) -> the internal shape the harness reads ({'figures', 'answers', 'decode_errors'})\ncheck(family, passages, calc) -> {'errors': [(rule, detail)], 'recorded': [...], 'published': {view_id: [low, high, None]}}\n\"\"\"\nimport itertools\nimport math\nimport re\n\nKINDS = {'source', 'computed', 'estimate'}\nBASES = {'reported', 'guidance', 'consensus', 'third_party', 'forecast'}\nEXTERNAL = {'consensus', 'guidance', 'third_party'}\nUNITS = {'USD bn', '%', 'x'}\nARITY = {'sum': (2, None), 'product': (2, None), 'difference': (2, 2), 'ratio': (2, 2), 'share': (2, 2), 'growth': (2, 2),\n         'apply_growth': (2, 2)}\nQUALIFIERS_BEFORE = sorted(['more than', 'at least', 'up to', 'as much as', 'less than', 'over', 'about', 'approximately', 'around',\n                            'roughly', 'nearly', 'almost', 'below', 'under', 'near'], key=len, reverse=True)\nQUALIFIERS_AFTER = ['or more', 'or less']\nQUALIFIERS = ['none'] + QUALIFIERS_BEFORE + QUALIFIERS_AFTER\nSCALE = {'trillion': 1000.0, 'tn': 1000.0, 't': 1000.0, 'billion': 1.0, 'bn': 1.0, 'b': 1.0, 'million': 0.001, 'mn': 0.001, 'm': 0.001}\nLIMIT = 1e9                                              # [54] a number beyond this is refused, not computed\nMAX_RANGE_LEAVES = 12\n   475\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is in {parsed[\"unit\"]}, not {f[\"unit\"]}'))\n   476\t            continue\n   477\t        if 'low' in parsed:\n   478\t            ok = (_same(f.get('low'), parsed['low']) and _same(f.get('high'), parsed['high'])) or \\\n   479\t                 ('mid' in parsed and _same(f.get('value'), parsed['mid']))\n   480\t        else:\n   481\t            ok = _same(f.get('value'), parsed['value'])\n   482\t        if not ok:\n   483\t            want = (f'low {parsed[\"low\"]:g} and high {parsed[\"high\"]:g}' + (f', or value {parsed[\"mid\"]:g}' if 'mid' in parsed else '')\n   484\t                    if 'low' in parsed else f'value {parsed[\"value\"]:g}')\n   485\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" gives {want} {parsed[\"unit\"]}'))\n   486\t            continue\n   487\t        q = f.get('qualifier') or 'none'\n   488\t        written = []\n   489\t        for start, end in starts:\n   490\t            before, after = text[:start], text[end:]\n   491\t            found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_BEFORE if re.search(r'(?:^|\\W)' + re.escape(w) + r'\\s*$', before, re.I)), None)\n   492\t            if found is None:\n   493\t                found = next((w for w in QUALIFIERS_AFTER if re.match(r'^\\s*,?\\s*' + re.escape(w) + r'\\b', after, re.I)), None)\n   494\t            written.append(found or 'none')\n   495\t        if q not in written:\n   496\t            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: the quote writes \"{written[0] if written else \"none\"}\" with \"{qn}\", but the qualifier is {q}'))\n   497\t    return errors\n   498\t\n   499\t\n   500\tdef _arithmetic(figs, calc):\n   501\t    errors = []\n   502\t    for f in figs.values():\n   503\t        if f['kind'] != 'computed':\n   504\t            continue\n   505\t        fid, op, ins = f['id'], f['operation'], f['inputs']\n   506\t        unit = _result_unit(op, [figs[i]['unit'] for i in ins])\n   507\t        if unit is None:\n   508\t            hint = ' (use share for a whole times a percentage)' if op == 'product' else ''\n   509\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: {op} cannot take inputs in {\", \".join(figs[i][\"unit\"] for i in ins)}{hint}'))\n   510\t            continue\n   511\t        if unit != f['unit']:\n   512\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: {op} of these inputs gives {unit}, not {f[\"unit\"]}'))\n   513\t            continue\n   514\t        got = {i: calc.interval(i) for i in ins}\n   515\t        if not all(got.values()):\n   516\t            continue                                       # an input that cannot be evaluated is refused at that input\n   517\t        if op in ('ratio', 'growth'):\n   518\t            denominator = got[ins[1]] if op == 'ratio' else got[ins[0]]\n   519\t            if denominator[0] <= 0 <= denominator[1]:\n   520\t                errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: divides by a figure whose value can be zero'))\n   521\t                continue\n   522\t        if op == 'share' and not (0 <= got[ins[1]][0] and got[ins[1]][1] <= 100):\n   523\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: a share takes a percentage from 0 to 100'))\n   524\t            continue\n   525\t        value = calc.interval(fid)\n   526\t        if value is None:\n   527\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: the value cannot be evaluated'))\n   528\t            continue\n   529\t        lo, hi = value\n   530\t        if _is_range(f):\n   531\t            ok = _close(f['low'], lo, unit) and _close(f['high'], hi, unit)\n   532\t        else:\n   533\t            ok = _close(f['value'], (lo + hi) / 2, unit)\n   534\t        if not ok:\n   535\t            declared = f'{f[\"low\"]:g}–{f[\"high\"]:g}' if _is_range(f) else f'{f[\"value\"]:g}'\n   536\t            got_text = f'{lo:.4g}' if lo == hi else f'{lo:.4g}–{hi:.4g}'\n   537\t            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: declared {declared} but {op} of {\", \".join(ins)} gives {got_text}'))\n   538\t    return errors\n   539\t\n   540\t\n   541\tdef _overlaps_band(interval, band):\n   542\t    lo, hi = interval\n   543\t    b_lo = band[0] if band[0] is not None else -math.inf\n   544\t    b_hi = band[1] if band[1] is not None else math.inf\n   545\t    if lo == hi:\n   546\t        return b_lo <= lo < b_hi\n   547\t    return lo < b_hi and hi >= b_lo\n   548\t\n   549\t\n   550\tdef _answers(family, figs, calc, answers):\n   551\t    errors, outcomes = [], {}\n   552\t    members = family['members']\n   553\t    for a in answers:\n   554\t        v = a.get('view_id')\n   555\t        member = members.get(v)\n   328\t- **One class at a time** (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"). Count the misses by mechanism; take the largest\n   329\t  class; trace it to the sentence that causes it; change one thing aimed at that class; run the whole bench (a prompt change alters every\n   330\t  request) against the previous version; read every regression to its raw answer. The pages that showed the failure locate its\n   331\t  mechanism; only the whole bench (every set, every draw) accepts the change.\n   332\t- **Name the effect on the judgement first.** Every proposed change states the effect it is expected to have on a reader or on the final\n   333\t  judgement, and is tested there where possible; a step's own counts do not decide it alone. A fix that keeps moving its error to\n   334\t  neighbouring cases stops being tuned on that set (Alex, 2026-11-09: \"感觉现在一直在修一些mknor的原地打转，你是不是要思考下现在具体还差什么high priority big impact 的需要做？\";\n   335\t  he approved this rule the same day, D391).\n   336\t- **A model call is a sampler.** Re-send a stored request before calling its answer a defect. Totals and acceptance come from at least\n   337\t  three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A direct-read pass\n   338\t  (one send per article, every answer read beside its source; D238) locates mechanisms; its counts are one draw and are labelled so.\n   339\t- **A written log.** One row per arm: hypothesis, change, numbers against the control (regressions first), kept or not. Refuted arms and\n   340\t  your own mistakes stay in it; the next session starts from its last row.\n   341\t- **Own the loop, within what Alex agreed.** When the direction is agreed and no stop is in force (`CURRENT_STATE.md`), push your own\n   342\t  hypotheses through $0 checks and probes of a few cents on stored cases without asking. A registered test, an end-to-end run, or\n   343\t  anything that departs from the agreed plan starts on his word; a result that contradicts the hypothesis stops the line and goes to him\n   344\t  (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\"). While he is not active (D439), \"his word\" is the council's\n   345\t  recorded consensus, and a stopped line goes to the council for re-assessment.\n   346\t- **Council before every change is sent.** A prompt, schema, code or criterion change, including one found by reading a probe, is a new\n   347\t  arm. It goes to an independent Codex read and a challenge round before its first send, and its result goes to a challenge round\n   348\t  before the next change. Reading the traces and the model's own account locates a fix; it does not review it (Alex, 2026-11-08:\n   349\t  \"你这些改动有我你有好好的跑council 再下手\").\n   350\t  Every round's reasoning and decisions are reflected on in the council until there is a consensus; the decision is recorded, and\n   351\t  the next test goes straight ahead, held to the PRD and its quantified goals, with every trace read by you yourself and not only\n3:  \"case\": \"P\",\n4:  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n7:  \"errors\": [],\n59:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n62:  \"case\": \"P\",\n63:  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n66:  \"errors\": [],\n129:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n132:  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n133:  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n136:  \"errors\": [],\n214:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n217:  \"case\": \"P-v4\",\n218:  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n221:  \"errors\": [],\n265:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n268:  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n269:  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n272:  \"errors\": [],\n355:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n358:  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n359:  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n362:  \"errors\": [],\n424:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n427:  \"case\": \"S\",\n428:  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n431:  \"errors\": [],\n484:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n487:  \"case\": \"S\",\n488:  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n491:  \"errors\": [],\n541:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n544:  \"case\": \"O\",\n545:  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n548:  \"errors\": [],\n586:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n589:  \"case\": \"O\",\n590:  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n593:  \"errors\": [],\n641:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n644:  \"case\": \"E\",\n645:  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n648:  \"errors\": [],\n676:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n679:  \"case\": \"E\",\n680:  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n683:  \"errors\": [],\n720:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.claude.json\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.claude.json\nrg -n 'e5014 |e1416 |e5376 |e4082 |e1649 |e2458 |e4508 ' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt | cut -c1-230\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.codex.json\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q08.claude.json\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.codex.json\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/adjudication/R07.claude.json\nrg -n 'e5014 |e1416 |e5376 |e4082 |e1649 |e2458 |e4508 ' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt | cut -c1-230\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"The answer uses $745 billion in total capex, the high end of e5014, and assumes 90% goes to data centers based on e5014 and the Meta comparison in e1549 and e3686. That yields $670.5 billion, within rung 3's $600 billion to under $700 billion band.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F13 = F11 x F12 = 745 x 90% = 670.5, inside rung 3 (600 to below 700). F11 (745 total capex) is a stated assumption: the top of Zacks's roughly 720-745 range (e5014), below the 760 plan (e2458). The company guidance in view backs it (Alphabet 195-205 e1254, Amazon 220 e1649, Meta 125-145 e1549, which leaves about 175-205 for Microsoft), and the slippage reasons cite e122 and e5292. F12 (90% data-center share) is the writer's own stated assumption, and the answer says the packet has no group-wide split. Its only grounds are Meta's 130 AI capex (e3686) against its 125-145 guidance (e1549), a ratio of about 90-104%, and Zacks's 'large portion' wording (e5014). The rung depends on this assumption: at a share of about 94% or more, the same 745 total lands in rung 4. The answer names the spending mix as its biggest uncertainty near that cutoff.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"Zacks supports $745 billion in total spending, describing a “large portion” as directed toward AI infrastructure. Applying the stated 90% share gives $670.5 billion, which is in rung 3. But rung 3 requires a share below about 94%, and the cited passages give no reason to expect the four companies’ share to fall below that cutoff. Meta’s $130 billion in AI spending is about 96% of the midpoint of its total spending guidance, and the AWS growth passage gives no spending breakdown. The 90% assumption is explicit, but the evidence does not support the deduction that puts the forecast in rung 3.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": []\n     9\t}     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"why\": \"The rung rests entirely on F12's 90% data-center share: share(745, 90) = 670.5, rung 3, but rung 3 holds only while the share is below 700 / 745 = 94.0%. The shown passages put the share near 100%, not 90%: e1947 says the four 'will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year', against the same four's 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion' of capital expenditures (e5014; e2079), which is at least 98%; NVIDIA's top-five figure is written both as total 'CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers ... nearly $800 billion in 2026' (e2759) and as 'nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026' (e5376), matching BofA's five-company total capex of 'roughly $795 billion' (e5055); e4082 puts 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year' and e1416 says tech giants 'will spend an estimated $750 billion this year'. The answer's own stated basis, Meta's '$130 billion of AI capex' (e3686) against its '$125 billion to $145 billion' guidance (e1549), gives 90% to 104%, about 96% at the midpoint, which is above the 94% cutoff. The cut to 90% to 'allow for other capital spending' has no passage behind it (e4404 says only that 'Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited'), so the forecast below $700 billion does not follow from the passages; with any share the passages support (94% or more), the same F11 of $745 billion gives rung 4. The arithmetic is correct and the share is written as its own figure as the format rule asks ('write the deduction or share as its own figure'), so no format defect arises; the failure is that the decisive assumption is not supported.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n17:- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in cale\n24:- e1649 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:275ccde6edc6d12657c32c3e 2026-08-06] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projection from $200 billion to $220 billion; the article says the inc\n37:- e2458 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The top four hyperscalers ; from gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23] The top four hyperscalers plan to spend $760 billion on capital expenditures in calendar\n47:- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in \n83:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 bill\n128:- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capita\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Q10|Q06|Q12' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/{codex-readers.log,Q10.codex-prompt.txt,Q06.codex-prompt.txt,Q12.codex-prompt.txt} | cut -c1-250\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json | sed -n '6,40p'\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json | sed -n '9,23p'\nrg -n '\"'^#'\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R01.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'Q10|Q06|Q12' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/{codex-readers.log,Q10.codex-prompt.txt,Q06.codex-prompt.txt,Q12.codex-prompt.txt} | cut -c1-250\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-adjudication/Q10.codex.json | sed -n '6,40p'\nnl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex.json | sed -n '9,23p'\nrg -n '\"'^#'\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R01.md\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.codex-prompt.txt:1:Read the file /Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q12.md and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow the brief at its top: \npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.codex-prompt.txt:1:Read the file /Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q06.md and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow the brief at its top: \npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/codex-readers.log:4:Q06 exit 0\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/codex-readers.log:8:Q12 exit 0\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/codex-readers.log:14:Q10 exit 0\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.codex-prompt.txt:1:Read the file /Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow the brief at its top: \n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F6\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"F6 quotes e2652's full expression, '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%'. The format permits that expression to be 'given as low and high or its midpoint as value'. The $108 billion point is the midpoint.\"\n    15\t    },\n    16\t    {\n    17\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    18\t      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n    19\t      \"figures\": [\"F15\", \"F11\", \"F13\"],\n    20\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    21\t      \"why\": \"The listed inputs give 128.36 × 93.5 ÷ 100 = 120.0166. F15's reported 120.01 differs by $0.0066 billion and satisfies the rule that 'The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %)'.\"\n    22\t    },\n    23\t    {\n    24\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 2\",\n    25\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    26\t      \"figures\": [\"F6\"],\n    27\t      \"decision\": \"reject\",\n    28\t      \"why\": \"F6 preserves '$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%' in quote_number and uses the permitted midpoint as value. The qualifier field is for 'the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or \\\\'or more\\\\' / \\\\'or less\\\\' just after)'. The quote has no such qualifier, so 'none' is correct.\"\n    29\t    }\n    30\t  ]\n    31\t}     9\t  \"defects\": [\n    10\t    {\n    11\t      \"type\": \"number misread\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F6\"],\n    13\t      \"detail\": \"e2652 gives $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%. F6 preserves that wording in the quote but records the guidance as an unqualified point with no bounds.\"\n    14\t    },\n    15\t    {\n    16\t      \"type\": \"wrong arithmetic\",\n    17\t      \"figures\": [\"F15\", \"F11\", \"F13\"],\n    18\t      \"detail\": \"The listed inputs give 128.36 × 93.5% = 120.0166, which rounds to 120.02 billion. The stated 120.01 billion can be reproduced by carrying unrounded upstream values, but the answer does not state that convention. The difference leaves the forecast in rung 2.\"\n    19\t    }\n    20\t  ]\n    21\t}1:# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, SMALL-DESIGN.md §5)\n32:# The request as the writer saw it\n169:# The answer\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md | sed -n '10,36p;62,65p;851,860p'; rg -n '\"'^#|e1947 |e3686 |e5014 '\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt | cut -c1-300; rg -n 'S \\\\| R01|R07|R11' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md | cut -c1-250\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q10.md | sed -n '10,36p;62,65p;851,860p'; rg -n '\"'^#|e1947 |e3686 |e5014 '\"' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt | cut -c1-300; rg -n 'S \\\\| R01|R07|R11' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md | cut -c1-250\"","aggregated_output":"    10\t \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n    11\t               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n    12\t\n    13\tDefinitions:\n    14\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    15\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage writes.\n    16\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs.\n    17\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    18\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    19\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    20\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    21\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value\n    22\t  of the quarter.\n    23\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    24\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    25\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    26\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    27\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    28\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    29\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of the question's quantity\n    30\t  (a ruling).\n    31\tReturn an empty \"defects\" list when you find none. Return only JSON.\n    32\t\n    33\t# The request as the writer saw it\n    34\t\n    35\t[Family] members:\n    36\tThesis v8 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Data Center segment revenue in FY2027\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n    62\t- e2652 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; from nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, with no Data Center compute revenue from China assumed.\n    63\t    Quote: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\n    64\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2670, e2748, e2818, e3009, e3782; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    65\t- e2686 [fact; Colette Kress; original source Amazon Web Services 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] AWS plans to deploy an additional 2 million NVIDIA GPUs, along with Vera CPUs, from Q2 FY2027 through Q2 FY2029.\n   851\t    },\n   852\t    {\n   853\t      \"id\": \"F6\",\n   854\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"NVIDIA\", \"measure\": \"revenue\", \"scope\": \"company\", \"period\": \"FY2027 Q3\"},\n   855\t      \"kind\": \"source\", \"basis\": \"guidance\", \"value\": 108, \"low\": null, \"high\": null, \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   856\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e2652\", \"quote_number\": \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\", \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n   857\t      \"inputs\": null, \"operation\": null, \"basis_text\": null, \"rests_on\": null,\n   858\t      \"outcome_for\": [], \"reference_for\": []\n   859\t    },\n   860\t    {\n29:- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in\n45:- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n83:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion dir\n17:- **Every case has a usable reply.** The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R02 and R11).\n45:- **R07 (S, Codex writer) is settled \"not supported\".** Its rung rests on a 90% data-center share, while the passages put the share near\n64:- **The readers did their job on substance.** They found the one reply whose decisive assumption is unsupported (R07). That is the\n69:  - R11's prose offers \"$138 billion in Q4\" as reaching rung 1, but it gives $428.32B against the $431.2B edge. The forecast itself\n74:  - R07's unsupported share.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c '\nimport collections,copy,importlib.util,json,pathlib,re\nbase=pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22'\\\\'')\nmod=pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py'\\\\'')\nspec=importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('\\\\''local_calc_check'\\\\'',mod)\ncs=importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\nspec.loader.exec_module(cs)\nkey=json.loads((base/'\\\\''reread-key.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nchecked=json.loads((base/'\\\\''CHECKED.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nassert len(checked)==12 and all(c['\\\\''stage'\\\\'']=='\\\\''checked'\\\\'' and c['\\\\''decision'\\\\'']=='\\\\''publish'\\\\'' and not c['\\\\''errors'\\\\''] for c in checked)\ndef data(pid):\n k=key[pid]\n d=json.loads((base/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']/'\\\\''item.json'\\\\'').read_text())\n a=json.loads((base/'\\\\''reread-packets'\\\\''/f'\\\\''{pid}.md'\\\\'').read_text().split('\\\\''\\\\n# The answer\\\\n\\\\n'\\\\'')[1])\n return d, a\ndef check(d,a):\n calc=cs.from_answer(a,d['\\\\''family'\\\\''])\n return cs.check(d['\\\\''family'\\\\''],d['\\\\''passages'\\\\''],calc)['\\\\''errors'\\\\'']\nfor pid in sorted(key):\n d,a=data(pid)\n errors=check(d,a)\n print(pid,key[pid],errors)\n assert {c for c,_ in errors}==({'\\\\''S6'\\\\''} if pid=='\\\\''Q06'\\\\'' else set())\nd,a=data('\\\\''Q10'\\\\'')\nf=next(f for f in a['\\\\''figures'\\\\''] if f['\\\\''id'\\\\'']=='\\\\''F6'\\\\'')\nf['\\\\''value'\\\\'']=108.5\nprint('\\\\''Suggested true misread control'\\\\'',check(d,a))\nassert [c for c,_ in check(d,a)]==['\\\\''S1'\\\\'']\nfor pid in ['\\\\''Q07'\\\\'','\\\\''Q09'\\\\'','\\\\''Q10'\\\\'','\\\\''Q14'\\\\'']:\n k=key[pid]\n text=(base/'\\\\''reread-adjudication'\\\\''/f'\\\\''{pid}.md'\\\\'').read_text()\n system,tail=text.split('\\\\''# The system text the writer was given\\\\n'\\\\'')[1].split('\\\\''# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)\\\\n'\\\\'')\n fmt,rest=tail.split('\\\\''# The request (questions, ladders and every passage)\\\\n'\\\\'')\n user,ans=rest.split('\\\\''# The answer\\\\n'\\\\'')\n p=base/k['\\\\''case'\\\\'']\n schema=json.loads((p/'\\\\''schema.json'\\\\'').read_text())\n def desc(s):\n  if isinstance(s,dict):\n   return ([s['\\\\''description'\\\\'']] if isinstance(s.get('\\\\''description'\\\\''),str) else [])+[d for v in s.values() for d in desc(v)]\n  if isinstance(s,list):\n   return [d for v in s for d in desc(v)]\n  return []\n assert system.strip()==(p/'\\\\''system.txt'\\\\'').read_text().strip()\n assert user.strip()==(p/'\\\\''user.txt'\\\\'').read_text().strip()\n assert json.loads(ans)==data(pid)[1]\n values=json.loads(fmt).values()\n assert collections.Counter(values)==collections.Counter(desc(schema))\n print(pid,'\\\\''adjudication source/answer matched'\\\\'',len(values),'\\\\''distinct description paths'\\\\'')\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
